1 NAME Willem III 'Gauwers' /van Nevele/ Seigneur d’Ostove/Oosthosen et Tannecy
2 GIVN Willem III 'Gauwers'
2 SURN van Nevele
2 NSFX Seigneur d’Ostove/Oosthosen et Tannecy
2 _MARNM van Nevele
1 SEX M
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} While Wilhelmine I provided the prestigious Halluin bloodline and the "Lichtervelde part," Willem III 'Gauwers' van Nevele provided the raw political muscle and the administrative engine. He was a high-ranking memb
2 CONC er of the House of Gavere, a family so powerful they were often called "the Princes of Flanders" in all but name.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''BIOGRAPHY: Willem III 'Gauwers' van Nevele'''
2 CONT Titles: Seigneur d’Oosthosen, de Tannecy (Tannay), and de Schuurvelde; Knight (Chevalier).
2 CONT Active: c. 1340 – 1383.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''I. The "Gauwers" Lineage: The House of Gavere'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Willem belonged to the Nevele-Gavere branch. The nickname "Gauwers" (or Gauwiers) is a linguistic evolution of "Gavere."
2 CONT
2 CONT The Nevele Link: As a younger son or member of a cadet branch of the lords of Nevele, he didn't inherit the main castle at Nevele. Instead, he was "carved out" a territory in Maritime Flanders (the north).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Arms: Or, a cross gules (A gold shield with a red cross). This is one of the most recognizable shields in the Armorial de Gelre.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''II. The Tannecy Consolidation (The "Caput")'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Willem’s primary historical contribution was the development of Tannecy (Tannay).
2 CONT
2 CONT Strategic Location: Tannecy sat near the Châtellenie of Cassel. In the 14th century, this was the "front line" of Comtal administration.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Oosthosen Connection: Oosthosen was likely a "motte" or a landed estate that provided the agricultural wealth (the "grain") to fund his political ambitions in the Count's court.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''III. Military and Administrative Service'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Willem lived through the most chaotic years of the Hundred Years' War and the Flemish revolts.
2 CONT
2 CONT Service to Louis de Mâle: He was a loyalist. While the weavers of Ghent were rebelling, Willem was part of the "Knights of the Count" who maintained order in the north.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Schuurvelde Claim: He was the legal holder of the Schuurvelde rights. When the Count confiscated Drincham, Willem was the "man on the ground" who ensured that the estate remained within his family's influence, eventually setti
2 CONC ng up the marriage of his daughter to the Count's son.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''IV. The Marriage to Wilhelmine I (The "Merger")'''
2 CONT
2 CONT His marriage to Wilhelmine I van Halluin was a masterstroke of regional consolidation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Halluin Influence: It gave him access to the southern nobility (Lille/Douai).
2 CONT
2 CONT Lichtervelde Interests: It brought the "Lichtervelde parts" into his portfolio, which his son would later use to claim the Barony.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''V. Death and the "1383 Pivot"'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Willem died roughly around 1383—the same year the Count granted Drincham to Jean "Sans Terre."
2 CONT
2 CONT The Timing: His death created a brief power vacuum that was immediately filled by the Count’s intervention. His widow, Wilhelmine I, took over the administration of Tannecy, and his daughter was married off to Jean "Sans Terre" to
2 CONC  keep the Drincham-Schuurvelde connection alive.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Summary for the "Paper" Search'''
2 CONT
2 CONT To find Willem III in the archives, the researcher should look for:
2 CONT *The 1370 Charter: Where he is likely mentioned alongside Rogier van Lichtervelde.
2 CONT *Série B (Comptes de Cassel): Records of "Relief" paid for the lands of Tannecy and Oosthosen between 1360 and 1380.
2 CONT *The "Gauwers" Epithet: He is often indexed under "Gavere" or "Gauwiers" rather than "Nevele."
2 CONT
2 CONT ''"Willem III 'Gauwers' was the architect of the Tannecy branch. He moved the family's focus from the interior to the strategic maritime corridor, providing the land and the administrative rank that allowed his son to marry a Gist
2 CONC el and his daughter to marry a Prince of Flanders. He is the 'Founder' of the Lichtervelde Barony line."''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT <span style="color:red; font-weight:bold;">This analysis and summary is drafted with AI assistance, cross-referenced with as supported by The 1383 Drincham Grant (B 104), The 1385–1400 Tannecy Dénombrements (B 1597–1598), The July
2 CONC  1401 Legitimation (B 156), The 1395–1410 Lichtervelde Relief (B 1603), and the Halluin Temporary Holding "Shadow Pedigree" Repository”, and other evidence documentation as referenced, etc. (The Summary is intended for leads only,
2 CONC  please check your sources.). Mistakes can be made.</span>
2 CONT ...
2 CONT II. Wilhelmine van Halluin (c. 1355 – post 1397)
2 CONT '''Spouse: Willem III «Gauwers» van Nevele (seigneur d’Ostove/Oosthosen et Tannecy).'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Historical Logic:''' The Nevele family was a major power in the region. Marriage to Willem III was intended to secure the Oosthosen and Tannecy manors—fiefs that were geographically clustered near the Lichtervelde estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The Confirmation:''' Because Wilhelmine survived until at least 1397 (post-dating her husband's death in 1388), she likely acted as a "Dowager Lady" over her husband’s holdings. This is a common pattern where widows maintained 
2 CONC control of sub-fiefs, which is why she is frequently titled Vrouw van Lichtervelde en Tannay in secondary records—she was holding a portion of the family estate as a dower.
