1 OCCU seigneur de Lalaing, Bugnicourt, Fressin et Hordain, Bailli de Hainaut, Sénéchal d’Ostrevant, Stadhouder de Hollande…
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Guillaume 1er de Lalaing d’abord dit «de Bugnicourt'''»
2 CONT ° ~1400 + 27/08/1475 (inh. à Lalaing) chevalier, seigneur de Lalaing, Bugnicourt, Fressin et Hordain, Bailli de Hainaut (1427-1433), Sénéchal d’Ostrevant (1441, par achat), Stadhouder de Hollande (1440-1445), Lieutenant-Général du
2 CONC  duc Philippe II de Bourgogne au duché de Luxembourg (1441), achète l’office de la «Vénerie héritable de Hainaut» (~1430, à Jean de Maubeuge ; officie qu’il revend ~1454 à Antoine Rollin, seigneur d’Aymeries), Chambellan & proche 
2 CONC du duc Charles «Le Téméraire» & chevalier d’honneur de la duchesse, 
2 CONT X 1468 siège de Liège
2 CONT
2 CONT ép. ~1418 Jeanne de Créquy «L’Aînée», dame de Bugnicourt + 21/10/1495 (fille de Jean IV, seigneur de Créquy, Canaples et Fressin, et de Jeanne de Roye ; veuve sans enfants de Robert de Wavrin, Sénéchal de Flandres +X 1415, Azincou
2 CONC rt)
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Pattou, Etienne. “Famille & Seigneurs De Lalaing.” Racines et Histoire :''
2 CONT __________
2 CONT '''Guillaume, seigneur de Lalaing and de Bugni''', 
2 CONT by Bugni(49) Bugni court, etc., seneschal of Ostrevant (by purchase in 1441), knight, bailiff of Hainaut from 1427 to 1433, stadtholder (governor) of Holland until 1445, lieutenant general of Duke Philippe le Bon in the duchy of L
2 CONC uxembourg in 1441 , chamberlain of the duke and knight of honor of the duchess, - married around 1418 Jeanne DE CRÉQUY, childless widow of Robert de Wavrin, son of the lord of Wavrin and killed with his father at Azincourt in 1415
2 CONC . Died in 1475, he lies in Lalain. During his father's lifetime, he was known as the Lord of Bugnicourt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1430, he acquired from Jean de Maubeuge the feudal office “of the heritable venerie of Haynnau”; but Duke Philip the Good expressed the "intention of taking it back for the money and applying it to the future of the country
2 CONC  of Haynnau"; however the prince abandoned this project, and in 1454, the Sire de Lalaing sold to "monsr Anthonne Raulin, lord of Aymeries", the liege fief "comprised of the inheritable venerie of Haynnau, also in the louuerie, in
2 CONC  pourseugs, in tax, in revenue of silver, dainty and cappons” (53).
2 CONT
2 CONT Guillaume de Lalaing and his wife, who lived until 1495, mourning the death of three sons and deploring the disorders of the only one who remained, had children of both sexes:
2 CONT 1° '''Jacques de Lalaing''', the Good Knight, born around 1420, at the castle of Lalaing, knight of the Golden Fleece in 1451, after his famous step of the Fountain of "plours", killed in front of Poucques, three leagues from Ghen
2 CONC t, on July 3, 1453, at the moment when the war against the rebellious Ghenters was about to end. He left an unblemished reputation and made his name illustrious for ever.
2 CONT 2° '''Jean de Lalaing''', who follows.
2 CONT 3° '''Philippe de Lalaing''', emulator of the Good Knight, born around 1430, killed at Montlhéry, on July 16, 1465. We published his step of the Perron fée, held in Bruges in 1463 (Douai, 1874, in-8°).
2 CONT
2 CONT He left several bastards:
2 CONT ..A. Méliador, bastard of Lalaing, squire of stable of the king of the Romans and of the archduke his son, bailli of Douai since 1489 until his death in 1499 (50); lying in the church of the cordeliers or walloon récollets of Doua
2 CONC i, in front of the altar Sainte-Barbe.
2 CONT It broke of a net of azure in bar.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married delle N.... DE N...., who bore: Gules with three fess argent (51); alias;
2 CONT Fasced of silver and gules of six parts (52).
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1500, the fief of La Prayelle in Contiches, belonging to the Motte d'Orchies, belonged to "the vesve of Méliador de Lallaing"; it had been, in 1473, to the knight Philippe de Wavrin, lord of. Saint-Venant, who became lord o
2 CONC f Wavrin after his father, around 1480, and died in 1500. They had :
2 CONT
2 CONT ….a. Artus de Lalaing, esquire, lord of La Prayelle, married to Jeanne Du BRUGANDIN, who married Jean Tauve de La Vacquerie, esquire, chief-eschevin (mayor), widower, with adult children, of Isabeau Gosson, in Douai, in October 15
2 CONC 36; she had died before 1548 and lay at Saint-Albin, in front of the altar of Saint John and Saint Michael; related (aunt perhaps) to Pierre du Brugandin, esquire, lord of the said place, to Hercules, to Barbe and to Louise du Bru
2 CONC gandin, brothers and sisters, alive in 1548.
2 CONT
2 CONT ….b. Jeanne de Lalaing (who broke a golden plain), wife of Maurand DE HAUSSY, esquire, lord of Remerchicourt, born around 1485, chief of Douai in 1548, 1552 and 1557, died on December 4, 1558 at the palace of Bassecourt of which h
2 CONC e was "concierge"; we would say today: governor; - son of Jean de Haussy, receiver of the domain in Douai (1491-1518), bailiff of that city (1504-1513), "concierge" of Bassecourt, ennobled in 1500, and "demoiselle Anthoine" Le Pre
2 CONC udhomme. They had two daughters, Antoinette and Jeanne de Haussy, who married into the du Hem d'Auby family.
2 CONT
2 CONT ….c. Philippote de Lalaing, widow in 1530 of Jacques DE TOUVILLE, squire, "familiar domesticq" of the count of Gavre, lying in Ghent, in the church of Saint-Michel. On her husband's tombstone, she bears: Argent with three fess of 
2 CONC Gules, with a full quarter of Lalaing (53); it was undoubtedly a combination of her father's and mother's coats of arms: at that time, the full arms put in a full quarter on any shield were a sign of bastardy. According to the gen
2 CONC ealogical table of the Cabinet des titres, she bore, like her two sisters, a golden plain,
2 CONT
2 CONT ….d. "Damoisello" N.... of Lalaing.
2 CONT
2 CONT ..B. Demoiselle A...., bastard of Lalaing (who broke like her brother and sister, according to the said genealogical chart), in 1499 widow of Antoine DE BOUBAIS. Of which posterity.
2 CONT
2 CONT ..C. Catherine, bastard of Lalaing, "by the late Sir Phle engendered or body of Jehenne de Gand, for then unmarried", as it is explained in the genealogical chart. as it is explained in the letters of legitimation that she obtaine
2 CONC d, for a fee, in Brussels, in September 1520 (54), and that she made register Lille, in the chamber of the accounts (20th register of the charters, f° vjxxj), on September 2, 1522 (55), but without discharging the 62 livres 10 sol
2 CONC s of the finance taxed by the chamber, so that the registration was declared "of null value. At the time of obtaining the letters of 1520, she was married to "Jehan, sr DE LA DEUZE". 
2 CONT
2 CONT 4° '''Antoine de Lalaing''', knight, lord of Bugnicourt, chamberlain of the duke, knighted at the coronation of King Louis XI in Rheims on August 15, 1461; he fought at Monthéry, alongside his brother Philippe (56). He would have 
2 CONC married Marie DE NOYELLES Wyon, heiress of Hangest, who married Gilles de Waziers, knight, lord of Heudincourt, according to the genealogists, who are without departure, reg. aux rémissions, B 1727, fo xxvij; in margin: " moiennan
2 CONC t fine ciuille "; it was the " finance " taxed in Lille by the chamber of the counted.
2 CONT
2 CONT It is doubtless more exact here than when they make Antoine de Lalaing die in 1477 under the blows of the Swiss while: he died on January 8, 1469 (old style), lying in the church of Lalaing, in the chapel of Notre-Dame de Sainghin
2 CONC ', where was a beautiful and large stone with its representation and its four quarters, which stone is now preserved in a private property of the village.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had from Catherine de couvringhe, "for then not married", a bastard:
2 CONT
2 CONT ..A. Rodigue, bastard of Lalaing in 1505, squire and captain of the archers of the body of the archduke Philip the Handsome, king of Castile, legitimized, about this time, by this prince, without financing, "for regard of the virt
2 CONC ues and good deaths are in him, and of the great, leases and pleasant services which he has, all his eaige, faiz to feuz our predecessors, whom God absoille, and, to us, and has fencoires daily" (57). Molinet quotes him several ti
2 CONC mes, about tournaments, military expeditions, etc. He broke with a net a few times. He broke a golden net in a bar (cabinet of titles; genealogical table).
2 CONT He left a daughter:
2 CONT
2 CONT ….a. Jeanne de Lalaing, died on November 28, 1540, - wife of Lambert DE LIGNE, bastard of Barbançon, esquire, who died on November 20, 1555, natural son of Michel de Ligne, lord of Barbançon, who died in 1468, - rested with her hu
2 CONC sband, in the church of Boussu, where their epitaph is (58)
2 CONT In the great genealogical table of the Cabinet des titres, drawn up towards the end of the 16th century, she is mentioned, with her alliance, but without filiation.
2 CONT
2 CONT 5° '''Yolente de Lalaing''', born around 1422, married to Renaud, sire DE BREDERODE, of the princely house of Holland, knight [of the Golden Fleece; whose posterity.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1492, widowed, she resided at the castle of Brederode (Molinet, IV, page 279).
2 CONT
2 CONT 6° '''Isabeau de Lalaing''', canoness of Mons in 1429, married Pierre DE HENNIN, lord of' Boussu, knight of the Golden Fleece; whose posterity, notably the counts of Boussu. Finally the lord Guillaume, seems to have had, before hi
2 CONC s marriage, a natural child:
2 CONT
2 CONT ..1° (bis)." '''Jehan de Lalaing''', b. [bastard; would it not be rather bailli?] of Haubourdin, lived in the year 1456, husband of Peronne GUILBAUT, daughter of the late Jehan"; he broke the shield of Lalaing with a bar of vert, 
2 CONC and his wife bore: Argent with a chevron of azure with an eagle of gules in the dexter canton (large genealogical table of the Cabinet of titles). The Guilbauts were from Lille. Another Péronne Guilbaut, daughter of Guy, treasurer
2 CONC  of the finances of the duke, died about 1430, was the first wife of Bauduin d' Ongnies, governor of Lille.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Brassart, Felix, Société Académique, editor. Souvenirs De La Flandre Wallonne,  Catalogue des Nobels de Nom, de Lalaing, Dix-Septieme, L. Crépin, 1882. un Comite Historique et Archeologique: Memories of Walloon Flanders: pp. 39-
2 CONC 47.
2 CONT __________
2 CONT '''GUILLAUME de Lalaing''', son of OSTE de Lalaing & his wife Yolande de Barbançon Dame de Hantes (-24 Aug 1475).  Seigneur de Bugnicourt, de iure uxoris.  Seigneur de Lalaing.  The testament of “Guillaume seigneur de Lallain, de 
2 CONC Bugnicourt, Fressaing, Hordaing, et seneschal d’Ostrevant”, dated 4 Sep 1473, donated property for the souls of “nous de Jehenne de Crequi notre...compaigne et de deffunts messires Jacques [...lequel gist en le chappelle de notre 
2 CONC Dame de Sainghin scitué en ladite eglise de Lallain], Philippe [...lequel termina vie par trespas en la bataille du Mont le Hevry] et Anthoine de Lallain [...lequel gist en ladite chapelle de notre Dame de Sanghien] nos enfants”, 
2 CONC approved by “nostre...filz aisne et heritier apparent maistre Jehan de Lalain”[512].  An epitaph at Lalaing records the burials of “Guillaume seigneur de Lalaing” who died 27 Aug 1473 and “Jehene de Crequy dame de Lalaing sa...epe
2 CONC use” who died 21 Oct 1495[513]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m (after 1415) as her second husband, '''JEANNE de Créquy Dame de Bugnicourt''', widow of ROBERT [VIII] Seigneur de Wavrin, daughter of JEAN [IV] Seigneur de Créquy & his wife Jeanne de Roye Dame d’Espourdin.  Goethals records her
2 CONC  parentage and two marriages, without citing the sources on which this information is based[514].  The testament of “Guillaume seigneur de Lallain...”, dated 4 Sep 1473, donated property for the souls of “nous de Jehenne de Crequi
2 CONC  notre...compaigne...”[515].  An epitaph at Lalaing records the burials of “Guillaume seigneur de Lalaing” who died 27 Aug 1473 and “Jehene de Crequy dame de Lalaing sa...epeuse” who died 21 Oct 1495[516]. 
2 CONT Guillaume & his wife had children: 
2 CONT 1.         '''JACQUES de Lalaing''' (-Poucques 3 Jul 1453, bur Lalaing).  An epitaph at Lalaing Sainte-Aldegonde records the burial of “Jacques de Lalaing aisné fils de...Guillaume seigneur de Lalaing” who died 3 Jul 1453 “au sièg
2 CONC e devant Ponckes”[517].  The testament of “Guillaume seigneur de Lallain...”, dated 4 Sep 1473, donated property for the souls of “...deffunts messires Jacques [...lequel gist en le chappelle de notre Dame de Sainghin scitué en la
2 CONC dite eglise de Lallain]...”, approved by “nostre...filz aisne et heritier apparent maistre Jehan de Lalain”[518]. 
2 CONT 2.         '''JEAN de Lalaing''' (-17 Dec 1498, bur Douai Cordéliers).  A charter dated 16 Jul 1468 records an enquiry into the property “délaissés par le trépas de feu messire Jacques de Lalaing”, with the consent of “messire Gui
2 CONC llaume seigneur de Lalaing, Jean et messire Antoine ses enfants”[519].  The testament of “Guillaume seigneur de Lallain...”, dated 4 Sep 1473, donated property for the souls of his three deceased sons, approved by “nostre...filz a
2 CONC isne et heritier apparent maistre Jehan de Lalain”[520].  An epitaph at Douai Cordéliers records the burial of “Jehan baron de Lalaing, seigneur de Bugnicourt, Fressaing, Monchicourt, Hourdain et de Brébières, sénéchal d’Ostrevant
2 CONC ” who died 17 Dec 1498[521].  Mistress: CATHERINE Wiette, daughter of ---.  A Généalogie de Croy et de Lalaing, written in [1566], records that “Jehan de Lalaing, iie fils de messire Guillamme et de la fille de Crequy...“ lived wi
2 CONC th “une...fille au monnier de Lalaing” by whom he had four sons and one daughter “Blance”[522].  The Catalogue des Lalaing lists these illegitimate children and their descendants[523]. 
2 CONT 3.         '''PHILIPPE de Lalaing''' (-killed in battle Montlhéry 16 Jul 1465).  The testament of “Guillaume seigneur de Lallain...”, dated 4 Sep 1473, donated property for the souls of “...deffunts messires...Philippe [...lequel 
2 CONC termina vie par trespas en la bataille du Mont le Hevry]...”, approved by “nostre...filz aisne et heritier apparent maistre Jehan de Lalain”[524]. 
2 CONT 4.         '''ANTOINE de Lalaing''' (-8 Jan 1470, bur Lalaing).  The testament of “Guillaume seigneur de Lallain...”, dated 4 Sep 1473, donated property for the souls of “...deffunts messires...Anthoine de Lallain [...lequel gist 
2 CONC en ladite chapelle de notre Dame de Sanghien] nos enfants”, approved by “nostre...filz aisne et heritier apparent maistre Jehan de Lalain”[525].  An epitaph at Lalaing records the burial of “Anthoine de Lalaing seigneur de Bugnico
2 CONC urt chevalier...frère maisné [de]...Jacques” who died 9 Jan 1469[526]. 
2 CONT 5.         '''YOLANDE de Lalaing''' (-after 1492).  The Catalogue des Lalaing records her parentage and marriage[527].  m REINALD Heer van Brederode, son of WALRAM Heer van Brederode & his wife Johanna van Vianen (-1473). 
2 CONT 6.         '''ISABELLE de Lalaing''' ).  The Catalogue des Lalaing records her parentage and marriage[528].  m PIERRE de Hénin Seigneur de Boussu, son of ---. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Cawley, Charles. “NORTHERN FRANCE-CAMBRAI, DOUAI, VALENCIENNES.” Medieval Lands, Foundation for Medieval Genealogy, 24 Mar. 2022, https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/nfracado.htm#_Toc44147371''
