2 NICK Sansse
1 NAME Sanche de Lalaing, seigneur d'Operbaix
1 SEX M
1 DEAT
2 DATE 1460
1 OCCU seigneur d'Operbaix! canon of Saint-Amé de Douai and archdeacon of Ostrevant, seigneur of Bricques and Thilly, squire
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Notes 
2 CONT Ou Sanson ou Sausse. Il vécut 108 ans. Chevalier. Seigneur d'Opprebais. Chambellan du duc de Bourgogne. Prévôt à Valenciennes (1437-1455). Il fut dans sa jeunesse chanoine de Saint Amé à Douai et archidiacre d'Ostrevant.
2 CONT Source : J de Saint-Genois - Monumens anciens,1806, L de Herckenrode, 1868, F Brassart, 1879, de Herckenrode, 1844.
2 CONT Death: F Brasssart, 1879.
2 CONT F. Brassart: He broke three lions of.... (perhaps those of Barbancon, because of his mother) in the first diamond (42)
2 CONT
2 CONT _____
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Sanche (Sanse, Sanson) de Lalaing''' + ~1460 chanoine de Saint-Amé de Douai, Archidiacre d’Ostrevant, écuyer, seigneur de Bricques et de Thilly, puis chevalier (17/04/1436, Paris), seigneur d’Oprebaix, Chambellan du duc de Bour
2 CONC gogne, Prévôt (-Le-Comte) à Valenciennes (1437-1455), Bailli de Cambrai (1439)
2 CONT (brise de 3 lions (de sinople ?) au 1° losange)
2 CONT
2 CONT ép. Catherine de Robersart, dame de Bruille et d’Escaillon
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Pattou, Etienne. “Famille & Seigneurs De Lalaing.” Racines et Histoire :''
2 CONT __________
2 CONT ''Sanche de Lalaing'', knight (in Paris, April 17, 1436), lord of Oprebaix, chamberlain of the Duke of Burgundy, provost-the-count in Valenciennes (1437-1455), died around 1460, husband of Catherine DE ROBERSART , by whom he had a
2 CONC  daughter, their only heiress: Jeanne de Lalaing, "demoiselle d'Escaillon et de Bruille," in 1461, under the tutelage of the lord of Lalaing, his uncle, married Philippe DE BOURBON, lord of Duisant, of the house of France, who was
2 CONC  widowed of her, in 1475, with children. According to Carpentier (History of Cambray, II, page 713), the lord and lady of Oprebaix would also have had, in particular: Alix de Lalaing, nun in the abbey of Prémy; but we know how muc
2 CONC h the assertions of this author are subject to caution. Sanche (or Sansse, Sanson, but not Sausse, as it has often been printed) was, in his youth, canon of Saint-Amé de Douai and archdeacon of Ostrevant. He was qualified in 1436 
2 CONC as "Sansse de Lalaing, lord of Bricques and Thilly, squire" (Departmental archives, county of the grand bailliage of Hainaut, September 1, 1435 - August 31, 1436, f° 43 V°). His name is honorably cited by the chroniclers alongside
2 CONC  the illustrious Simon, his brother. He broke three lions of.... (perhaps those of Barbancon, because of his mother) in the first diamond (42). He signed: “Sanson de Lalaing.
2 CONT
2 CONT (42) Le P. Anselme, I, p. 361, donne pour armes à Catherine (sic) de Lalaing, fille de Sanche, un écartelé de Lilaing et " d'argent à trois lions de sinople, armés et couronnez d'or. » Ce sont les armes de Barbancon, sauf que les 
2 CONC lions sont de gueules..
2 CONT
2 CONT Brassart, Felix, Société Académique, editor. Souvenirs De La Flandre Wallonne,  Catalogue des Nobels de Nom, de Lalaing, Dix-Septieme, L. Crépin, 1882. un Comite Historique et Archeologique: Memories of Walloon Flanders: pp. 34-35
2 CONT __________
2 CONT '''SANSON [Sanche?] de Lalaing''' (-[1460]).  “Sansse de Lalaing seigneur de Bricques et de Thilly escuier” was named in accounts of the grand bailliage de Hainaut dated 1436[497].  Seigneur d’Oprebaix[498].  Goethals records that
2 CONC  “Sausse de Lalaing” and his wife “Agnes de Robersart” are recorded in a window at Valenciennes église des Carmes, decorated with the shields of “Lalaing, Barbançon, Robersart, Mastaing. Montigny, Rassenghien, ---, Trazegnies”[499
2 CONC ]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT  m '''CATHERINE [Agnès?] de Robersar'''t, daughter of ---.  The Catalogue des Lalaing names “Catherine de Robersart” as the wife of Sanche de Lalaing without providing any other details[500].  Brassart names her “Catherine de Robe
2 CONC rsart dame d’Ecaillon et de Bruille” (no source cited)[501].  Goethals names her “Agnes d’Ostrevant dite de Robersart” without citing the source on which this information is based[502].  Was she related to John/Jean Robsart/Robers
2 CONC art who was born in Hainaut, campaigned in France with Henry V King of England, was granted “the baronies of S. Saviour le Visconte and Neahou in Normandy”, made “Captain of Caudebec” during the reign of King Henry VI, and was bur
2 CONC ied at Christ Church, London (epitaph recording the burial of “Dom. Johannes Robsard valens miles in armis” who died 24 Dec 1450)?[503].  Goethals records that “Sausse de Lalaing” and his wife “Agnes de Robersart” are recorded in 
2 CONC a window at Valenciennes église des Carmes, decorated with the shields of “Lalaing, Barbançon, Robersart, Mastaing. Montigny, Rassenghien, ---, Trazegnies”[504].  Sanson & his wife had one child: 
2 CONT
2 CONT i)          '''JEANNE [Catherine?] de Lalaing''' ([after 1445?]-[before 1475 or after 1477]).  Père Anselme records her parentage (naming her “Catherine”) and marriage, noting that she was living in 1477, without citing the source
2 CONC  which confirms this information[505].  The Catalogue des Lalaing records that “Jeanne de Lalaing demoiselle d’Escaillon et de Bruille”, only daughter and heiress of “Sanche de Lalaing Seigneur d’Oprebaix, chambellan du duc de Bou
2 CONC rgogne, prévôt-le-comte à Valenciennes” (“mort vers 1460”) and “Catherine de Robersart” was in 1461 “sous la tutelle du seigneur de Lalaing son oncle” and that her husband was “veuf d’elle en 1475”[506].  No primary source has bee
2 CONC n identified which reconciles the difference in name and date of death.  Her birth date is estimated from her still being a minor in 1461.  
2 CONT
2 CONT m (after 1461) '''PHILIPPE de Bourbon Seigneur de Duisant''', son of JEAN de Bourbon Seigneur de Carency & his second wife Jeanne de Vendômois (-after 20 Jul 1492). 
