1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_de_la_Cerda
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Charles d'Espagne''' born 1326, murdered at l'Aigle, France, 6 Jan 1354
2 CONT
2 CONT He succeeded his father in 1327 as Seigneur de Lunel (part).  He was the favourite of Jean II King of France.  Connétable de France 1346, and 1350.  The king installed him as Seigneur de Benon, de Fontenay-L'Abatu, d'Archai, de La
2 CONC  Roche, de Cognac et d'Agoux, and Comte d’Angoulême, replacing Carlos II King of Navarre.  Governor of Picardie and Artois.  He was murdered at the auberge "La Truite qui file" at L'Aigle on the orders of the King of Navarre who w
2 CONC as jealous of his influence over the King.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Married:
2 CONT #Marguerite de Blois-Châtillon (c. 1339-c. 1354), in 1351, daughter of Charles de Blois-Châtillon Seigneur de Guise Comte de Penthièvre [claimant to the Duchy of Bretagne] & his wife Jeanne de Bretagne-Penthièvre Duchess de Bretag
2 CONC ne.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles de La Cerda (1327 – 8 January 1354, L'Aigle), (in French: Charles d'Espagne) was Franco-Castilian nobleman and soldier, was the son of Alfonso de la Cerda (died 1327) and Isabelle d'Antoing.
2 CONT
2 CONT A boyhood companion and favorite of John, Duke of Normandy, he commanded the Castilian galleys at the Battle of L'Espagnols-sur-Mer, where he was defeated by Edward III of England after a long and desperate struggle. Soon after Jo
2 CONC hn's accession as John II of France, he was appointed Constable of France and created Count of Angoulême. Vacant since the death of Joan II of Navarre in 1349, the title to Angoulême was claimed by her son, Charles II of Navarre, 
2 CONC who bitterly resented La Cerda's preferment. In 1351, he married Marguerite, daughter of Charles of Blois.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1354, ruffians hired by the King of Navarre set upon and slew de la Cerda in an inn.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Carlos de España (1326 - l'Aigle, 6 de enero de 1354), señor de Lunel en 1327, Condestable de Francia en 1347, y conde de Angulema en 1352, así como gobernador de Picardía y de Artois. Casó en 1351 con Margarita de Bretaña, señora
2 CONC  de Aigle (Normandía), hija de Carlos de Châtillón-Blois, duque de Bretaña, y de Juana de Bretaña, condesa de Penthiève. Murió sin descendenia, asesinado, supuestamente por orden de Carlos II de Navarra
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Carlos de La Cerda, Carlos de España, comte d'Angoulême
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------
2 CONT
2 CONT -http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/CASTILE.htm#Charlesdied1354
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         CHARLES d'Espagne ([1326]-murdered l'Aigle 6 Jan 1354).  The testament of “Alfonsus de Ispania dominus de Lunello miles”, dated 15 Apr 1327, bequeathed revenue from “terre mee...in Navarra” to “genitor meus” until “Karo
2 CONC lus filius meus” reached 16 years of age, with the consent of “Isabellis...consortis mee”[1427].  He succeeded his father in 1327 as Seigneur de Lunel (part).  He was the favourite of Jean II King of France.  Connétable de France 
2 CONC 1346, and 1350.  The king installed him as Seigneur de Benon, de Fontenay-L'Abatu, d'Archai, de La Roche, de Cognac et d'Agoux, and Comte d’Angoulême, replacing Carlos II King of Navarre.  Governor of Picardie and Artois.  “Hugues
2 CONC  de Melun sire d’Antoing, d’Epinoy et châtelain de Gand et Marguerite de Picquegny son épouse” confirmed letters, issued by “Jehan vicomte de Melun chambellan de France sire de Monstruel et Belloy et d’Isabelle dame d’Antoing et d
2 CONC ’Epinoy vicomtesse de Melun, père et mère du dit Hugues” and signed by “son frère Charles d’Espaigne seigneur de Lunel” dated 26 Jul 1347, relating to a foundation in the church of Antoing by charter dated 10 Jul 1369[1428].  He w
2 CONC as murdered at the auberge "La Truite qui file" at L'Aigle on the orders of the king of Navarre who was jealous of his influence over the king of France.  The Chronique des règnes de Jean II et de Charles V records that “monseigne
2 CONC ur Charles roys de Navarre et contes d’Evreux” ordered the killing of “monseigneur Charles d’Espaigne, lors connestable de France” 8 Jan 1353 (O.S.) “en la ville de Laigle en Normandie, en une hostelerie”[1429].  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''m''' (1351) MARGUERITE de Blois-Châtillon, daughter of CHARLES de Blois-Châtillon Seigneur de Guise Comte de Penthièvre [claimant to the Duchy of Bretagne] & his wife Jeanne de Bretagne-Penthièvre Dss de Bretagne ([1339]-[1354]
2 CONC ).  The Chronique des règnes de Jean II et de Charles V records the marriage in 1351 of “monseigneur Charles d’Espaigne lors connestable de France...[conte] d’Angoulesme” and “la fille monseigneur Charles de Bloise duc de Bretaing
2 CONC ne”[1430]. 
