2 CONT
2 CONT Jean I de Croÿ
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Jump to: navigation, search
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean I de Croÿ, Seigneur de Croÿ et d'Araines, Baron de Renty et de Seneghem (around 1365 - Agincourt on October 25, 1415) was the founder of the House of Croÿ .
2 CONT
2 CONT His parents were Guillaume, seigneur de Croÿ (+1384) and Isabeau de Renty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean I de Croÿ was responsible for the ascendancy of his family to a position of supreme power in medieval Burgundy. He served Philip the Bold and his son John the Fearless in the capacity of councillor and chamberlain.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1384 he married a wealthy heiress, Marie de Craon ca (1370-1420), successfully suing her first husband's family upon her death. In 1397, Jean acquired the lordship of Chimay, which was to become a core dominion of the Croÿ fami
2 CONC ly. Four years later, he was appointed Governor of Artois and led the ducal armies against the rebellious citizens of Liege. He was recorded as the Grand-Bouteiller of King Charles VI of France in 1412, when he laid siege to Bourg
2 CONC es.
2 CONT
2 CONT The following year, Isabeau of Bavaria had him apprehended and incarcerated in the castle of Montlhéry, from where he escaped.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean, together with two of his sons, was killed in the Battle of Agincourt on October 25, 1415.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had seventeen children, amongst whom :
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine I de Croy, Comte de Porcéan (1385-1475)
2 CONT
2 CONT Archambaud (1386 - 1415 at Agincourt)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean (1387 - 1415 at Agincourt)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean II de Croÿ (1395 - 1473), Prince of Chimay, progenitor of the line of Croÿ-Solre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Agnes de Croÿ (1386 ?), mistress of John the Fearless Duke of Burgundy, by whom she had a natural son, John of Burgundy (Jean de Bourgogne), the future Bishop of Cambrai.
2 CONT
2 CONT Forrás / Source:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean_I_de_Croÿ
2 CONT
