1 OCCU Chevalier, Governor of Lille, Douai & Orchies;                       Councillor & Chamberlain to Philip the Good;                       Lord of Estrées, Gouy, Gruson, and Wambrechies
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ''At the administrative and courtly heart of the Burgundian state, the military governance of Lille and the ducal chamberlainship were held by lords who bound regional dominion to strategic blood alliances across F
2 CONC landers and Artois.''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Baudouin dit 'd'Estrées' d’Ongnies; Seigneur d’Estrées, de Gouy, et de Gruson; Chevalier'''
2 CONT
2 CONT (Verified Generation 19 Spouse)
2 CONT '''I. VITAL SUMMARY'''
2 CONT Birth Name: Baudouin d’Ongnies (dit "d'Estrées")
2 CONT Titles: Seigneur d’Estrées, de Gouy, et de Gruson; Chevalier
2 CONT True Generation Count: Generation 19 Spouse (contemporaneous with the children of Jan IV van Halewijn)
2 CONT Floruit (fl.) Dates: fl. c. 1420 – † 12 June 1459
2 CONT Status: High Courtier and Military Governor; Ducal Chamberlain
2 CONT Strategic Role: Regional Governance & Courtly Link (Governor of Lille, Douai, and Orchies; Councillor and Chamberlain to Philip the Good)
2 CONT Parents: Colart d’Ongnies, Seigneur d’Estrées & Marie de Molembais
2 CONT Primary Seat: Château d’Estrées / Governance Seat at Lille
2 CONT
2 CONT '''II. MARRIAGE AND ALIGNMENT'''
2 CONT Baudouin d’Ongnies married twice into prominent regional houses:
2 CONT First Marriage: Péronne Guilbaut, Dame de Bruay (-en-Artois), Ligny, et Quesnoy († 10 March 1439, buried at Lille), daughter of Gui Guilbaut, Chevalier and hereditary lord of Ligny and Bruay.
2 CONT Second Marriage: Isabeau (Isabelle) van Halewijn, Dame de Beaurepaire († 1475), senior daughter of Jan IV van Halewijn, Lord of Zwevegem, and Jacqueline de Ghistelles.
2 CONT His second marriage to Isabeau van Halewijn formed a direct alliance between the ducal administrative seat at Lille and the dense West Flemish landed network of the House of Halewijn-Zwevegem.
2 CONT Children's Bloc (Issue)
2 CONT '''By 1st Wife (Péronne Guilbaut):'''
2 CONT Antoine (alias Gilles) d’Ongnies († 19 March 1478): Chevalier, Lord of Bruay and Ligny, Governor of Lille, Douai, and Orchies; m. ~1441 Jeanne de Brimeu, Dame de Chaulnes.
2 CONT Madeleine d’Ongnies: m. ~1441 Jean de Croix, Chevalier, Lord of Croix and Flers.
2 CONT '''By 2nd Wife (Isabeau van Halewijn):'''
2 CONT Léon d’Ongnies: Lord of Willeman; m. Jeanne de Beauvais.
2 CONT Isabeau d’Ongnies: m. Oste (Corneille) de La Barre, Lord of Mouscron.
2 CONT Philippe d’Ongnies († 1465 at Montlhéry): Lord of Quesnoy-sur-Deûle, Carver (écuyer tranchant) to the Count of Charolais, Grand Bailiff of Courtrai (1464–1465); m. Marguerite van Varssenaere.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''III. NARRATIVE BIOGRAPHY'''
2 CONT Baudouin d’Ongnies was born into the prestigious House of Ongnies-Estrées, the son of Colart d’Ongnies and Marie de Molembais. Rising through the ranks of the Burgundian court under Duke Philip the Good, Baudouin distinguished him
2 CONC self in both military command and administrative governance. By 1437, he was appointed Councillor, Chamberlain, and Master of the Household (maître d’hôtel) to Duke Philip, placing him at the operational core of the ducal realm.
2 CONT
2 CONT His administrative authority reached its peak through his appointment as Governor (Gouverneur et Souverain-Bailli) of the strategic châtellenies of Lille, Douai, and Orchies (and at times holding jurisdiction over Dixmude). In thi
2 CONC s position, Baudouin oversaw military defense, judicial appeals, and municipal order across Walloon Flanders during a pivotal era of Burgundian centralization.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the death of his first wife, Péronne Guilbaut, in 1439, Baudouin contracted a second marriage with Isabeau van Halewijn, Dame de Beaurepaire. This marriage merged his high court standing with the extensive West Flemish l
2 CONC anded influence of Jan IV van Halewijn. Baudouin governed until his death on 12 June 1459, leaving a powerful lineage divided into the senior branch of Bruay/Chaulnes and the junior Halewijn-descended branch of Willeman and Quesno
2 CONC y.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''IV. HERALDRY & ALLIANCE SHIELD'''
2 CONT * House of d’Ongnies (Paternal): Vert, a fess dancetty or (or regional variant quarterings of Ongnies-Estrées).
2 CONT * House of Halewijn (Marital - 2nd): Argent, three lions rampant sable, armed, langued, and crowned or.
2 CONT * Alliance Shield: Impaled arms displaying d’Ongnies (Vert, a fess dancetty or) on the dexter alongside Halewijn (three crowned black lions on silver) on the sinister.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''V. DOCUMENTS AND EVIDENCE'''
2 CONT * P. Anselme de Sainte-Marie: Histoire Généalogique et Chronologique de la Maison Royale de France, Tome III (1733), pp. 904–920 (Seigneurs d'Hallwin et du Gavre) and Ongnies lineage sections.
2 CONT * Bibliothèque Nationale de France (BnF): Gallica Digital Library, Fol-Lm3-398 A (1), entries for the House of Ongnies (Seigneurs d'Estrées & Chaulnes).
2 CONT * Ducal Accounts of Burgundy (Comptes de la Chambre des Comptes de Lille): Records detailing Baudouin d'Ongnies as Governor of Lille, Douai, and Orchies, and Chamberlain to Philip the Good (1437–1459).
2 CONT * Halewijn-Zwevegem Family Cartularies: Primary marriage settlements and dower records for Isabeau van Halewijn and her husband Baudouin d'Ongnies.
2 CONT * Inscriptions et Épitaphes de la Ville de Lille: Municipal church rolls and burial registers recording the Guilbaut and d'Ongnies funerary monuments.
2 CONT * Nobiliaire des Pays-Bas et du Comté de Bourgogne: Lineage compilations documenting the succession of the Lordship of Estrées, Gouy, and Gruson.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VI. CURATOR DISCLAIMER'''
2 CONT <span style="color:red; font-weight:bold;">DISCLAIMER: This profile represents the True Generation 19 Spouse (husband of Isabeau van Halewijn, daughter of Jan IV van Halewijn and Jacqueline de Ghistelles). Do not renumber this pro
2 CONC file based on secondary French calendar indexes. Critical Curation Note: Care must be taken when mapping Baudouin’s issue between his two wives: senior children (Antoine/Gilles and Madeleine) belong to Péronne Guilbaut, while juni
2 CONC or children (Léon, Isabeau, and Philippe) belong to Isabeau van Halewijn. Do not conflate Baudouin with his nephew or later namesakes of the House of Ongnies. Notice: AI-assisted biographical summary. Generative AI models can make
2 CONC  mistakes, hallucinate details, or conflate generational data; readers are advised to check and verify against primary archival sources.</span>
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ''And now, for the rest of the story...''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Decoding the Lord of Estrées and Gruson'''
2 CONT If you were a merchant traveling through the gates of Lille in 1445, you didn’t just tip your hat to "Mr. d'Ongnies." You bowed to a man whose very name was a stacked inventory of fortified manors, judicial seats, and political tr
2 CONC iumphs across the Burgundian borderlands.
2 CONT
2 CONT To modern eyes, a name like Baudouin d’Ongnies, dit 'd'Estrées', Seigneur d’Estrées, de Gouy, de Gruson, et de Wambrechies looks like an aggressive exercise in vanity. But in the 15th-century Franco-Flemish marches, every added sy
2 CONC llable was a deed written in stone, parchment, and blood.
2 CONT Here is the story behind the title string—and how a single courtier brought it all to the doorstep of the House of Halewijn.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Part I: The Bloodline (d'Ongnies)'''
2 CONT It all began in Oignies (Ongnies), a ancient lordship nestled between Douai and Lille. The House of d'Ongnies were not johnny-come-lately courtiers; they were ancient chivalric stock of Artois. But as medieval noble families grew,
2 CONC  a single manor couldn't support half a dozen ambitious sons. To survive, cadet branches had to carve out their own territorial identities—or marry into them.
2 CONT
2 CONT Enter Baudouin’s line: the cadet branch that refused to remain in the shadow of the primary Oignies seat.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Part II: The Principal Crown (d'Estrées)'''
2 CONT To distinguish his personal line from his cousins, Baudouin’s father, Colart d’Ongnies, secured the lordship of Estrées (Estrées-Cauchy / Estrées-lez-La Bassée).
2 CONT
2 CONT This wasn't just a patch of farmland; Estrées sat squarely along key military transit corridors. Possession of Estrées was so central to Baudouin’s identity that court scribes routinely dropped his actual surname altogether, recor
2 CONC ding him simply in the ducal rolls as Baudouin, dit 'd'Estrées'. When Duke Philip the Good summoned his trusted inner circle, it was "d'Estrées" who answered the call to serve as Master of the Household.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Part III: Collecting the Borderlands (de Gruson, Gouy, et Wambrechies)'''
2 CONT As Baudouin’s star rose at the Burgundian court, so did his appetite for territorial consolidation around Lille—the administrative heart of Walloon Flanders:
2 CONT '''Gruson:''' Positioned just east of Lille near the Bouvines battlefield, the lordship of Gruson gave Baudouin direct judicial authority right on the doorstep of the provincial capital.
2 CONT '''Gouy & Wambrechies:''' Added to his portfolio to secure lucrative feudal dues, river tolls, and agricultural rents along the Deûle river valley.
2 CONT
2 CONT By the time he was appointed Governor and Sovereign-Bailiff of Lille, Douai, and Orchies, Baudouin didn't just govern the region for the Duke—he personally owned a ring of manors encircling its key strongholds.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Part IV: The Final Piece—The Halewijn Interlock'''
2 CONT A man with a title string that spanned three provinces needed a marital alliance to match. After the death of his first wife, Péronne Guilbaut (who brought him the Artois lordships of Bruay and Ligny), Baudouin set his sights nort
2 CONC h toward West Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT He secured Isabeau van Halewijn, daughter of Jan IV, the formidable "Keeper of the Unified Inheritance."
2 CONT Isabeau brought to the marriage her own territorial prize: the lordship of Beaurepaire in Hainaut. When Baudouin d'Ongnies and Isabeau van Halewijn joined hands, they effectively linked the judicial clout of the Governor of Lille 
2 CONC with the massive landed wealth of the House of Zwevegem.
2 CONT When early modern genealogists like Père Anselme sat down to map this alliance 300 years later, they didn't just see a marriage—they saw a territorial empire condensed into a single line of text: Baudouin d’Ongnies-d’Estrées-Gruso
2 CONC n.
2 CONT
