2 DATE 3 MAR 1476
2 ADDR
3 CITY Grandson
3 STAE VD
3 CTRY Switzerland
1 OCCU Chevalier and 2 more occupations
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Notes
2 CONT Fut armé chevalier par le duc Charles lors du siège de Neuss en Allemagne le 23 mai 1475. Chambellan du Duc, capitaine d'une compagnie d'ordonnance, il fut tué lors du désastre de Granson (Suisse) le 2 mars 1476 (vs).
2 CONT Source : F Brassart, 1879.
2 CONT _____
2 CONT '''Jean de Lalaing''' +X 02/03/1477 
2 CONT X Battle of Grandson (Granson) 
2 CONT - chevalier (armé le 23/05/1475 au siège de Neuss, par le duc Charles) 
2 CONT - Chambellan du duc, 
2 CONT - capitaine d’une compagnie d’ordonnanice
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Pattou, Etienne. “Famille & Seigneurs De Lalaing.” Racines et Histoire'' :
2 CONT __________
2 CONT '''Jean de Lalaing''', knight, having been armed by the hand of Duke Charles, at the siege of Neuss in Germany, on May 23, 1475, the day of the battle against the emperor (Molinet, I, page 129). Chamberlain of the duke, captain of
2 CONC  an ordinance company, he was killed at the disaster of Granson, against the Swiss, on March 2, 1476 (old style).
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Brassart, Felix, Société Académique, editor. Souvenirs De La Flandre Wallonne, Catalogue des Nobels de Nom, de Lalaing, Dix-Septieme, L. Crépin, 1882. un Comite Historique et Archeologique: Memories of Walloon Flanders: pp. 60.'
2 CONC '
2 CONT __________
2 CONT '''JEAN de Lalaing''' (-killed in battle Grandson 2 Mar 1477)
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Cawley, Charles. “NORTHERN FRANCE-CAMBRAI, DOUAI, VALENCIENNES.” Medieval Lands, Foundation for Medieval Genealogy, 24 Mar. 2022, https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/nfracado.htm''
2 CONT __________
2 CONT Curator note: the battle of Granson is discussed on Wikipedia here: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Grandson
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000188990976848&size=large}}
2 CONT The Battle of Grandson between the Burgundians and the Swiss at Grandson, Switzerland on March 2, 1476. Illustration by Jacques Marie Gaston Onfray de Breville dit JOB (1858-1931) in “” Louis XI”””, 1905. Private Collection
2 CONT Photo credit
2 CONT Photo © Photo Josse / Bridgeman Images
