1 OCCU Dame de Boesinghe (by marriage)
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ''Through the House of Stavele, the Lords of Boesinghe gained a powerful matrimonial anchor in the Barony of Isenghien and the upper court of Habsburg Flanders.''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Johanna (Jeanne) van Stavele, Dame de Boesinghe'''
2 CONT
2 CONT (Generation 20 - Marital Alignment)
2 CONT '''I. VITAL SUMMARY'''
2 CONT Birth Name: Johanna (Jeanne) van Stavele (de Stavele)
2 CONT Known Titles: Dame de Boesinghe (by marriage)
2 CONT Life Dates: b. 22 June 1459 – † 29 December 1509
2 CONT Burial: Choir of St. Michael’s Parish Church, Boezinge (before the main altar)
2 CONT Status: High Noble Matriarch; Consort to the Grand-Bailiff of Ypres
2 CONT Parents: Jan IV van Stavele, Heer van Izegem (Isenghien), Glageon & Jeanne de Berlaymont
2 CONT Primary Seat: Kasteel van Boesinghe (Boezinge, West Flanders)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''II. MARRIAGE AND ALIGNMENT'''
2 CONT Johanna married Nicolaas (Colard / Coolaert) van Halewijn († 23 December 1528), Chevalier, Heer van Boesinghe ende Rousselarie, Chamberlain to Archduke Philip the Handsome and Emperor Charles V, and Grand-Bailiff (Groot-Baljuw) of
2 CONC  the City and Châtellenie of Ypres.
2 CONT This high-status union bound the House of Halewijn to the prestigious Lords of Stavele (Heeren van Izegem / Isenghien). Through Johanna's lineage, the Boesinghe cadet line solidified its position within the top tier of Habsburg-Bu
2 CONC rgundian court nobility, positioning their children for elite alliances across Flanders and Hainaut.
2 CONT Children's Bloc (Issue - Stem of the Lords of Boesinghe)
2 CONT Jakob (Jacques) van Halewijn († 21 April 1537): Chevalier, Heer van Boesinghe; m. Anna d'Ognies († 1543), continuing the principal Boesinghe lordship and lineage.
2 CONT Joris (Georges) van Halewijn († 1536): Renowned Flemish humanist scholar, diplomat, and Lord of Comines/Halewijn branch connections; m. Antonia van Sint-Aldegonde.
2 CONT Nicolaas (Colard) van Halewijn († 1546): Chevalier and regional landholder.
2 CONT Gillette (Gilline / Ghislaine) van Halewijn († c. 1554): Married Maximilian van Witthem, Vicomte de Sébourg.
2 CONT Catharina van Halewijn (b. c. 1479): Married into the regional nobility (Jacques de Hallewyn).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''III. NARRATIVE BIOGRAPHY'''
2 CONT Born on 22 June 1459 into the powerful House of Stavele, Johanna was the daughter of Jan IV van Stavele, Lord of Izegem (Isenghien) and Glageon, and Jeanne de Berlaymont. Her family ranked among the premier baronage of West Flande
2 CONC rs and Hainaut, maintaining deep connections to the ducal court of Burgundy. Her precise birth date was preserved directly in domestic birth registers and house cartularies kept by the Lords of Stavele & Izegem (Maison de Stavele)
2 CONC , which meticulously detailed the births and godparents of Jan IV's offspring.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her marriage to Nicolaas "Colard" van Halewijn brought immense prestige and courtly leverage to the expanding Boesinghe estate. As her husband rose through the ranks of Habsburg administration—becoming a trusted Councillor and Cha
2 CONC mberlain to Philip the Handsome and young Charles V, as well as the supreme magistrate (Grand-Bailiff) of Ypres—Johanna managed the central domestic, financial, and feudal affairs at Kasteel van Boesinghe.
2 CONT
2 CONT Johanna passed away on 29 December 1509 at the age of 50, nearly two decades before her husband. She was interred in the choir of St. Michael’s Parish Church at Boezinge. When Nicolaas died on 23 December 1528, he was laid to rest
2 CONC  beside her under a grand carved bluestone (arduinen) tomb displaying their joint quarterings (kwartierstaten) and heraldic shields, recorded by 16th-century heraldic chroniclers.
2 CONT IV. HERALDRY & ALLIANCE SHIELD
2 CONT House of Stavele (Paternal): Gules, a chevron or, accompanied by three roses argent (or regional heraldic quarterings of Stavele/Izegem).
2 CONT House of Halewijn (Marital): Argent, three lions rampant sable, armed, langued, and crowned or.
2 CONT Alliance Shield: Impaled arms displaying Halewijn on the dexter alongside the ancestral coat of arms of Stavele on the sinister.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''V. DOCUMENTS AND PRIMARY EVIDENCE FOR VITAL DATES'''
2 CONT * Primary Epigraphic Inscription (Death Date: 29 December 1509):
2 CONT The funerary inscription carved onto the bluestone (arduinen) tomb in the choir of St. Michael's Church at Boezinge explicitly establishes her death on 29 December 1509.
2 CONT 1. Transcribed by Cornelius Gailliard (c. 1520–1563) in his Grafschriftenboek: "In de voorseide choor licht mer Coolaert van Halewijn... ende jonckvrouw Johanna van Stavele zijn geselstede...
2 CONT 2. Published in Baron Jean de Béthune’s Epitaphes et monuments des églises et couvents de la Flandre and Ferdinand Van de Putte’s Geschiedenis van Boezinge / Sas van Boezinge.
2 CONT * Stavele Cartularies & Family Registers (Birth Date: 22 June 1459):
2 CONT Her exact birth date is documented in the house registers (Maison de Stavele) recording the children of Jan IV van Stavele and Jeanne de Berlaymont, codified in modern compendia like Étienne Pattou's Maison de Stavele (Racines & H
2 CONC istoire).
2 CONT * Père Anselme de Sainte-Marie: Histoire Généalogique, Tome III, p. 908 (Branche de Bouzinghen).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VI. CURATOR DISCLAIMER'''
2 CONT <span style="color:red; font-weight:bold;">DISCLAIMER: This profile represents Johanna van Stavele (1459–1509), wife of Nicolaas (Colard) van Halewijn and mother of Jakob, Joris, and Gillette van Halewijn. Critical Generational Re
2 CONC solution Note: Do not confuse Johanna van Stavele with her mother (Jeanne de Berlaymont) or her daughter-in-law (Anna d'Ognies, wife of her son Jakob). Ensure her birth date (22 June 1459, via Stavele cartularies) and death date (
2 CONC 29 December 1509, via Boezinge choir tomb) are preserved according to Flemish cartularies and church epigraphy. Notice: AI-assisted biographical summary. Generative AI models can make mistakes, hallucinate details, or conflate gen
2 CONC erational data; readers are advised to check and verify against primary archival sources.</span>
