1 OCCU Marital Consort of Generation 13 (Louise d'Halluin).
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ''A Picard Military Anchor & High-Prestige Consort: Operating as head of a major cadet branch of the ancient House of d'Ailly (Vidames d'Amiens), Antoine III d'Ailly, Seigneur de Varennes (Varennes-en-Croix), serve
2 CONC d as a pivotal military figure and estate administrator in the Somme basin. His marital union with Generation 13 main-stem daughter Louise d'Halluin forged a vital geopolitical bridge between the Vidamé d'Amiens and the royal-bloo
2 CONC ded Halluin-Maignelay network across Picardy.''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Antoine III d'Ailly, Seigneur de Varennes (Varennes-en-Croix)'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''I. Vital Overview'''
2 CONT Operational Name: Antoine III d'Ailly (Antoine de Ailly)
2 CONT Active Era (fl.): Late 15th to Early 16th Century (c. 1485–1530s). Active military commander, regional magistrate, and seigneurial lord along the Picard frontier.
2 CONT Status: Marital Consort of Generation 13 (Louise d'Halluin).
2 CONT Primary Landholdings & Caputs: Lordship and Castle of Varennes (Varennes-en-Croix, Picardy), Toutencourt, and associated fiefs in the Amiénois and Santerre regions.
2 CONT Strategic Role: Consolidated the d'Ailly-Varennes territorial footprint along the Somme-Ancre corridors while linking his house directly to the Valois-Burgundian court standing of the House of Halluin.
2 CONT Primary Surnames & Archival Aliases: d'Ailly, de Ailly, Seigneur de Varennes, Sire de Varennes-en-Croix, Sieur de Toutencourt.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''II. Lineage Data'''
2 CONT House Origin: House of d'Ailly (Branch of the Seigneurs de Varennes / Cadet line of the Vidames d'Amiens and Barons de Picquigny).
2 CONT Parents: Senior line of the House of d'Ailly-Varennes.
2 CONT Marital Alliance: Married (Gen 13) Louise d'Halluin, Dame de Varennes (daughter of Jean II de Halluin x Jeanne de La Clite).
2 CONT Generational Sibling Block (House of d'Ailly-Varennes):
2 CONT Antoine III d'Ailly (Primary male heir; Sr. de Varennes et de Toutencourt)
2 CONT (Collateral d'Ailly siblings holding regional military commands and ecclesiastical prebends in Picardy)
2 CONT Primary Offspring Footprint:
2 CONT Succeeding d'Ailly-Varennes Line: Children who continued the senior Varennes-en-Croix descent, maintaining feudal alliances with the Picard and Artois nobility.
2 CONT Jurisdictional Footprint: Seigneurial Court of Varennes-en-Croix, Bailiwick of Amiens, and regional military registers of Picardy.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''III. Narrative Biography: Sentinel of the Ancre Valley'''
2 CONT Antoine III d'Ailly belonged to one of Picardy's most illustrious feudal dynasties. As Seigneurs de Varennes (Varennes-en-Croix), his branch held significant strategic lands along the Ancre river valley, serving as crucial buffers
2 CONC  for the nearby city of Amiens. His marriage to Louise d'Halluin represented a key regional power alignment, uniting the ancient lineage of d'Ailly with the rising political favor of the House of Halluin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Antoine III maintained an active presence across the Somme basin during the reign of Louis XII and Francis I. His position as Seigneur de Varennes required managing local feudal levies, maintaining castle defenses, and executing r
2 CONC oyal administrative decrees. Supported by his brother-in-law, François de Hallwin, Bishop of Amiens, and his brother-in-law Antoine d'Halluin, Governor of Péronne, Antoine III helped form a tightly integrated family nexus that dom
2 CONC inated the military and ecclesiastical governance of 16th-century Picardy.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''IV. Heraldry & Sigillography'''
2 CONT Armorial Bearings: Ancestral shield of the House of d'Ailly, occasionally brisured for the Varennes cadet line.
2 CONT Blazon: Gules, two chef-d'étau or, accompanied by two branches of alisier argent, a chef chequy argent and azure (d'Ailly).
2 CONT Context: Official seals from his chancery at Varennes display the distinctive chef échiqueté (chequy chief) of d'Ailly, frequently impaling the three crowned sable lions of Halluin on joint marital deeds with Louise.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''V. The Rest of the Story: Preserving the Varennes Patrimony'''
2 CONT The lordship of Varennes-en-Croix sat at a crossroad of regional trade and military transit. Managing the estate involved overseeing agricultural rents, feudal court jurisdictions, and defensive readiness during frequent border te
2 CONC nsions between the French Crown and the neighboring Low Countries.
2 CONT
2 CONT Through joint transactions with Louise d'Halluin, Antoine III expanded the d'Ailly-Varennes landholdings into neighboring parishes, securing dower releases and acquiring satellite fiefs such as Toutencourt. His governance ensured 
2 CONC that the Varennes-en-Croix caput remained a stable and prosperous domain, passing intact to his successors and solidifying the d'Ailly-Halluin heritage among the premier noble houses of the Amiénois.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VI. Primary Sources & Evidence Ledger'''
2 CONT * Archives Départementales de la Somme (Amiens - Série E & 1G): Feudal registers, land surveys (terriers), and notarized agreements for the lordship of Varennes-en-Croix and Toutencourt.
2 CONT * Père Anselme (1726–1733): Histoire généalogique et chronologique de la maison royale de France (Vol. VIII). Documents the d'Ailly-Varennes pedigree and confirms Antoine III's marriage to Louise d'Halluin.
2 CONT * Bibliothèque Nationale de France (Paris - Cabinet des Titres): Charters and letters patent referencing Antoine d'Ailly, Sire de Varennes, in 15th/16th-century Picardy muster rolls.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VII. Forensic Rationale & Research Notes'''
2 CONT Distinction from Collateral Namesakes: Care must be taken to distinguish Antoine III d'Ailly, Sr. de Varennes, from earlier and later namesakes in the House of d'Ailly, such as Antoine d'Ailly, Sr. de Tilloloy, or Antoine d'Ailly,
2 CONC  Vidame d'Amiens. Primary land records explicitly tie this node to Varennes-en-Croix and his marriage with Louise d'Halluin.
2 CONT Varennes Toponymy: In archival records, Varennes is frequently styled Varennes-en-Croix to differentiate it from other French locations named Varennes, anchoring this branch directly to the department of the Somme near Toutencourt
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VIII. Research & Category Verification'''
2 CONT Category A (Charter Verified): Parentage/lineage in the d'Ailly-Varennes branch, title of Seigneur de Varennes (Varennes-en-Croix), and marriage to (Gen 13) Louise d'Halluin.
2 CONT Category B (Traditional Lore / Peerage Syntheses): Minor details concerning individual troop levies during local Picardy border watch cycles.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''IX. Project Guardrails & Profile Advisory'''
2 CONT <span style="color:red; font-weight:bold;">DISCLAIMER: Artificial intelligence tools and automated peerage compilations can make mistakes or synthesize conflicting genealogical lore. This profile is structured to safeguard the Gen
2 CONC eration 13/14 main-stem and collateral nodes against accidental merges, chronological errors, and unverified traditional claims.</span>
2 CONT '''Verified Relationships:''' Records Antoine III d'Ailly (Seigneur de Varennes) as the marital consort of (Gen 13) Louise d'Halluin and brother-in-law to Philippe, Jean, Antoine, and François de Hallwin.
2 CONT '''Geographic Context:''' Anchored in Picardy (Varennes-en-Croix, Toutencourt, Amiens, Somme Valley).
2 CONT '''Updating Policy & Independent Verification:''' Users should independently verify critical data against primary archival sources. Any edits to primary charters, vital dates, land tenure, or matrimonial contracts must be supporte
2 CONC d by contemporary 16th-century records, notarized estate deeds, or official state/diocesan archives, and reviewed against project standards.
