2 _PRIM Y
2 NOTE description outlines the precise mechanism of matrilineal inheritance and coat-of-arms adoption in Ancien Régime heraldry.
3 CONT When a noble family's main male line was on the verge of extinction, the sole female heiress could require her suitor to "relever le nom et les armes"—take on the surname, heraldry, and feudal motto of her house—as an explicit con
3 CONC dition of the marriage contract so that the family identity would not die out.
3 CONT How the House of Albert d'Ailly was Formed
3 CONT The Heiress (House of d'Ailly):
3 CONT Claire Charlotte Eugénie d'Ailly was the sole heiress of the senior branch of the House of d'Ailly (Vidames d'Amiens and Seigneurs de Picquigny).
3 CONT The Marriage Contract (1620):
3 CONT She married Honoré d'Albert (1581–1649), Marshal of France and younger brother of Charles d'Albert, 1st Duke of Luynes. Under the terms of the marriage contract, Honoré was required to adopt the name, titles, and arms of the House
3 CONC  of d'Ailly.
3 CONT The Creation of the Title (1621):
3 CONT In 1621, King Louis XIII elevated their united lordships into a peerage duchy, creating Honoré d'Albert the 1st Duke of Chaulnes (duc de Chaulnes). This established the House of Albert d'Ailly.
3 CONT Understanding Their Combined Shield
3 CONT Because Honoré belonged paternally to the House of Albert (whose arms are Or, a lion rampant gules) and adopted the arms of d'Ailly (Gules, two branches of service tree in saltire argent, a chief chequy argent and azure), their ne
3 CONC w cadet branch combined the two:
3 CONT The Outer Field: Displays the arms of d'Ailly.
3 CONT The Center Inescutcheon (écusson sur-le-tout): Displays the arms of Albert.
3 CONT Summary for Antoine III d'Ailly
3 CONT Antoine III d'Ailly (fl. 1505–1520), husband of Louise d'Halluin, lived a full century before this 1620 marriage between Honoré d'Albert and Claire Charlotte d'Ailly.
3 CONT Antoine III belonged to the pure paternal line of d'Ailly.
3 CONT His profile should use the plain d'Ailly shield without the small lion emblem (inescutcheon) in the middle!
