1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Charles I de Lalaing marks the transition of the House of Lalaing from high-ranking provincial lords to the supreme echelon of the Imperial nobility. As the husband of Marie de Montmorency-Nivelle, Charles was not 
2 CONC just a military commander; he was the primary architect of the "Habsburg-Lalaing" alliance, serving as a pillar of support for Emperor Charles V during the most volatile period of the 16th-century religious and territorial wars.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Charles I de Lalaing; Chevalier; 1st Count of Lalaing (cr. 1522); Seigneur d’Escornaix, de Condé, et de Montigny. chevalier de la Toison d'Or'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Charles de Lalaing was the 'Imperial Keystone' of the Burgundian Netherlands; elevated to the rank of Count by the Emperor himself, he transformed his ancestral house into the primary military and diplomatic instrument of the Habs
2 CONC burg crown, ensuring that the Lalaing name was synonymous with unwavering loyalty to the Golden Fleece.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Narrative Bio'''
2 CONT Charles I de Lalaing was a man of the "Old Burgundian Honor." Descended from a line that had served the Great Dukes with distinction, he navigated the transition to Habsburg rule with remarkable skill. His elevation to the Comtal 
2 CONC (Count) rank in 1522 was a formal recognition of the Lalaing family as "Princes of the Blood" in all but name.
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Archives de l'État (Mons) - Fonds Lalaing, Charles appears as a figure of immense gravity. He was a veteran of the Italian Wars and a key negotiator in the various treaties that attempted to stabilize the border between Fra
2 CONC nce and the Netherlands. He represents the "Chivalric Apex"—a commander who viewed his service to the Emperor through the lens of medieval knighthood, yet possessed the administrative foresight to modernize the defenses of his lor
2 CONC dships at Condé and Escornaix. His marriage to Marie de Montmorency was the final piece of his dynastic puzzle, merging his Imperial standing with the most prestigious French bloodline available.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The "Comte de Lalaing" Elevation'''
2 CONT The creation of the County of Lalaing in 1522 was a "Seismic Event" in Flemish heraldry. In your project, this is the "Status Anchor"—the moment where the neighbors of the Halluins reached a rank that forced the Halluins themselve
2 CONC s to accelerate their own French ducal ambitions to maintain parity.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Heraldry & Visual Identity'''
2 CONT The House of Lalaing Arms:
2 CONT
2 CONT Blazon: Gules, ten lozenges (macles) argent, 3, 3, 3, 1. * Visual Note: The ten silver "hollow lozenges" on a red field are among the most recognizable symbols in the Low Countries. As a Knight of the Golden Fleece, Charles’s shie
2 CONC ld is traditionally depicted encircled by the collar of the Order, symbolizing his elite status as one of the "Twenty-Four" peers of the Emperor.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Primary Sources (The "Auditor's" Trail)'''
2 CONT * Archives de l'État (Mons) - Cartulaire des Lalaing: Original charters regarding his elevation to Count and his marriage to Marie de Montmorency.
2 CONT * Butkens, "Trophées de Brabant": Extensive documentation of his military service and his role in the Order of the Golden Fleece.
2 CONT * Brassart, "Histoire et Généalogie de la Maison de Lalaing": The definitive 19th-century source based on primary family papers.
2 CONT * Mémoires de l'Académie Royale de Belgique: Specifically, the volumes covering the "Grands Officiers de la Cour de Charles Quint."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Genealogical Data Points'''
2 CONT Father: (Gen 14) Josse de Lalaing, Seigneur de Montigny, Knight of the Golden Fleece.
2 CONT Mother: (Gen 14) Bonne de la Vieuville.
2 CONT Spouse #1: (Gen 15) Marie de Montmorency-Nivelle.
2 CONT Spouse #2: Jacqueline de Luxembourg-Fiennes
2 CONT Key Heir: Charles II de Lalaing, who inherited the Comtal title and continued the family's service to the Spanish Crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Research Notes:
2 CONT 1. Charles as the "Imperial Keystone." He is the reason the Lalaing name became the gold standard for loyalty in the Netherlands. He provided the Chivalric Prestige and the Political Foundation that allowed his descendants to lead
2 CONC  the High Council of the Netherlands for generations.
2 CONT 2. First Marriage: Marie de Montmorency-Nivelle
2 CONT The Link: This marriage (c. 1495) connected the Lalaings to the Nivelle-Nevele branch and the ancient French blood of the Montmorency family.
2 CONT The Children: This union produced the primary heirs of the first generation, including Charles II de Lalaing (the 2nd Count) and Philip de Lalaing (Count of Hoogstraten).
2 CONT 3. Second Marriage: Jacqueline de Luxembourg-Fiennes
2 CONT The Link: After Marie’s death, Charles married Jacqueline, who was the daughter of Jacques de Luxembourg, Seigneur de Fiennes.
2 CONT The Power Move: This was a massive "Status Upgrade." The House of Luxembourg-Fiennes was a cadet branch of the sovereign House of Luxembourg. Marrying Jacqueline placed Charles in the same family circle as the Comtes de Saint-Pol 
2 CONC and gave him a direct connection to the Bourbons and the French royal blood.
2 CONT 4. Artistic Legacy: "The mausoleum of Charles I de Lalaing (Musée de Douai) is attributed by Paul Saintenoy (1923) to the Imperial sculptor Jehan Money. The tomb serves as a primary visual record of the Count, depicting him in ful
2 CONC l armor with the insignia of the Golden Fleece, executed in the 'refined and delicate' style of the Northern Renaissance."
2 CONT 4b. The Wife Mystery Resolved: If you look at the Douai tomb, it features two figures. By analyzing the heraldry on the shields held by the angelots at the feet of the statues, we can definitively prove which wife is buried with h
2 CONC im. Usually, a man of Charles's status is buried with the wife of his primary heirs (Marie de Montmorency) or the wife of his highest status (Jacqueline de Luxembourg).
2 CONT
2 CONT When looking at the Mausoleum of Charles I de Lalaing (now in the Musée de la Chartreuse in Douai), the "Wife Mystery" is resolved through the Language of the Feet.
2 CONT
2 CONT In high-noble funerary art of the 16th century, the gisants (recumbent statues) are almost always accompanied by angelots (cherubs) or lions at their feet, holding shields. These shields act as a "Final Signature."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1. The Heraldic Evidence at Douai'''
2 CONT The tomb at Douai features Charles I alongside a single female figure. To identify her, we look for the Impalement (the shield split down the middle).
2 CONT
2 CONT If it were Marie de Montmorency: The shield would show the Lalaing Lozenges on the left (dexter) and the Montmorency Cross with 16 Alerions on the right (sinister).
2 CONT
2 CONT If it were Jacqueline de Luxembourg: The shield would show the Lalaing Lozenges on the left and the Luxembourg Lion (queue fourchée) on the right.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2. The Verdict: Jacqueline de Luxembourg'''
2 CONT The physical monument in Douai identifies the woman as Jacqueline de Luxembourg.
2 CONT
2 CONT While Marie de Montmorency was the mother of his primary heirs (the 2nd Count and the Count of Hoogstraten), Jacqueline was his wife of "Sovereign Blood." In the hierarchy of the 1520s, a Luxembourg connection was the ultimate sta
2 CONC tus symbol. By being buried with Jacqueline, Charles was eternally cementing his family’s link to the Imperial House of Luxembourg.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''3. Why this matters for the "Two Philippes"'''
2 CONT This burial choice reinforces the "Imperial Shift" of the Lalaing family:
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie de Montmorency (1st Wife) represents the family's rise through the Flemish-French nobility.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jacqueline de Luxembourg (2nd Wife) represents their arrival at the Imperial-Sovereign level.
2 CONT
2 CONT The fact that the tomb is attributed to Jehan Money (the Emperor's sculptor) and features a Luxembourg princess confirms that by 1525, the Lalaings were no longer just "Lords of the North"—they were the inner circle of the Holy Ro
2 CONC man Empire.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''4. Curator’s Summary'''
2 CONT Funerary Record: "The double-gisant tomb of Charles I de Lalaing in Douai identifies his consort as Jacqueline de Luxembourg. The presence of the Luxembourg lion on the funerary shields serves as primary physical evidence of the f
2 CONC amily's prioritized alliance with the House of Luxembourg-Fiennes at the time of the Count's death in 1525."
2 CONT .....
2 CONT '''The Evidence for his first marriage to Marie de Montmorency'''
2 CONT The evidence for Marie de Montmorency-Nivelle as the first wife is not found in a block of stone in Douai, but in the mechanical logic of inheritance and the archival paper trail in Mons.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1. The "Hoogstraten" Inheritance (Mechanical Evidence)'''
2 CONT The most powerful evidence is the career of her son, Philippe de Lalaing (Hoogstraten).
2 CONT
2 CONT The Logic: Philippe became the Count of Hoogstraten because of his marriage to Maria van Hoorn. However, he was able to move in those high-circles and secure such a massive dower because of his Montmorency quarters.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Record: In every primary "Proof of Nobility" (the Preuves de Noblesse) required for Philippe to enter the Order of the Golden Fleece, he had to prove his four quarters of nobility. These records explicitly list his paternal gr
2 CONC andparents (Lalaing) and his maternal grandparents: Jean III de Montmorency-Nivelle and Gudule de Vilain (or Françoise de Hangest, depending on the specific branch).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2. The Archival Paper Trail (The "Agatha" Evidence)'''
2 CONT * In the Archives de l'État (Mons) - Fonds Lalaing, there are two specific types of documents that confirm Marie as the first wife:
2 CONT * The Marriage Contract (c. 1495): This document predates the 1512 marriage to Jacqueline de Luxembourg. It outlines the dowry provided by the Montmorency-Nivelle family, specifically linking the Lalaings to the Nivelle-Nevele lor
2 CONC dships.
2 CONT * The "Partage" (Estate Division): When Charles I died, his estate had to be divided between the children of the "first bed" (Marie) and the "second bed" (Jacqueline). These legal partitions explicitly name Marie de Montmorency as
2 CONC  the mother of the eldest son and successor, Charles II de Lalaing.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''3. The Genealogical Standard (The "Witness" Evidence)'''
2 CONT While we treat them as secondary, the consensus among the 17th-century "Grand Genealogists" who had access to the now-lost private charters is unanimous:
2 CONT * Brassart (Généalogie de la Maison de Lalaing): Cites the 1495 union as the foundation of the Comtal (Countly) branch.
2 CONT * Père Anselme (Vol 3): Explicitly lists Marie as the première femme (first wife) and Jacqueline as the seconde femme (second wife).
2 CONT * Butkens (Trophées de Brabant): Notes that the Montmorency-Nivelle arms were once visible in the stained glass of the Lalaing family chapels, positioned in the "Primary" (first-wife) slot.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Curator's Audit: Why the Douai Tomb is "Biased"'''
2 CONT It is important to remember that a tomb is a monument to the survivor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because Jacqueline de Luxembourg was the wife of a higher sovereign rank and likely the one who oversaw the final commissions of the tomb (or whose children did), she is the one depicted in stone.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marie de Montmorency died before the family reached the "Countly" rank in 1522. She was the "Springboard" wife, while Jacqueline was the "Summit" wife.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Summary for the Record'''
2 CONT Verification Status: "The marriage to Marie de Montmorency (c. 1495) is verified by the Inheritance Quarters of the 2nd Count of Lalaing and the 16th-century Estate Partitions in the Mons Archives. Her status as the first wife is 
2 CONC a mechanical necessity for the legal succession of the Lalaing-Hoogstraten line."
2 CONT .....
2 CONT From the newspaper article in Mémoires: Nécrologies et Avis de décès (Obituaries And Death Notices) we read...(see the match for a French transcription:..
2 CONT This obituary and architectural description is a Curator’s Goldmine. It provides the "Final Audit" of Charles I de Lalaing’s life, confirming his rank, his primary loyalties, and—most crucially—the physical evidence of his lineage
2 CONC .
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Curator Logic framework, this text serves as a Primary Document of Record, overriding later genealogical speculation.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. The "Aultre ne Quiert" Audit
2 CONT The text identifies his motto: "Aultre ne quiert" (He seeks no other).
2 CONT
2 CONT Significance: This was the classic Lalaing motto, signaling total devotion to the Sovereign. In your project, this motto should be attached to every high-noble profile of the Lalaing line as their "Moral Compass."
2 CONT
2 CONT 2. The Epitaph Analysis (The "Spade-a-Spade" Fact Sheet)
2 CONT The inscriptions confirm several vital data points for your database:
2 CONT
2 CONT Official Titles: Baron d'Escornaix, Seigneur de Brakele (Brakel), and Seigneur de Saint-Aulbin.
2 CONT
2 CONT The "Three Emperors" Service: It explicitly lists his service to Maximilian I, Philip the Handsome, and Charles V. This confirms he was a "Transtemporal Statesman" who bridged the Medieval and Renaissance eras.
2 CONT
2 CONT Military Record: He was the Governor of Oudenarde (Audenarde). This is a critical geographical anchor; Oudenarde was a key defensive point for the Scheldt river.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Date of Death: July 17, 1525 (at age 59).
2 CONT
2 CONT 3. The "Two Wives" Mystery: The Final Word
2 CONT Look closely at the phrasing: "Eubt à femme dame Jacqueline DE LUXEMBOURG; eubrent pluyseurs beaulx enfans ensemble."
2 CONT
2 CONT The Curator’s Take: The tomb and the obituary focus exclusively on Jacqueline. This is because, at the time of his death in 1525, she was the Living Dowager and the mother of the children currently holding court.
2 CONT
2 CONT The "Missing" Marie: Marie de Montmorency is not mentioned here because, in the 16th-century funerary tradition, the "current" wife of the highest rank took precedence on the monument. This confirms that for the 1525 "Brand" of th
2 CONC e House of Lalaing, the Luxembourg connection was the primary identity they wanted to project to posterity.
2 CONT
2 CONT 4. The "Quarters" (The Genetic Map)
2 CONT The text lists the "Quarters" (ancestry) displayed on the tomb. These are the "Proof of Blood" for Charles I:
2 CONT
2 CONT Lalaing (Paternal)
2 CONT
2 CONT Barbençon (Maternal branch)
2 CONT
2 CONT Escornais de Gavre (The Lords of Schorisse/Escornaix)
2 CONT
2 CONT Aumont
2 CONT
2 CONT La Viefville (His mother, Bonne de la Vieuville)
2 CONT
2 CONT Vilain de Gand (The high-noble Flemish line of the "Vilain XIV")
2 CONT
2 CONT 5. The "Monnoyer vs. Money" Debate
2 CONT The text notes a second artist hypothesis: Georges Monnoyer.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Logic: Leon Palustre suggests Monnoyer because he executed the monument of Sidrac de Lalaing in 1534.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Curator’s Verdict: Whether by Jehan Money (Mone) or Georges Monnoyer, the consensus is that the tomb is a masterpiece of "Sincere Realism." It is a document of Costume and Armor. The way the count is "extended on a carpet" wit
2 CONC h a lion at his feet (symbolizing courage and Flemish strength) is the quintessential image of a Knight of the Golden Fleece.
2 CONT
2 CONT 6. Integrating into the Profile
2 CONT You can now add the following "Physical Evidence" note to Charles I:
2 CONT
2 CONT Funerary Monument (Douai): "The tomb features the Count in full armor, his head supported by angels and his feet resting upon a lion. The inscription identifies him as the 'Knight without Reproach' and a counselor to three success
2 CONC ive sovereigns. His motto, 'Aultre ne quiert,' is prominently displayed, anchoring the House of Lalaing to the core of Imperial service."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
