1 OCCU Dame de Boesinghe (by marriage); Dowager of Saint-Omer-Wallon-Capelle
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ''A noblewoman of West Flanders whose two marriages wove the ancient coastal lordships of Diksmuide and Saint-Omer into the rising power of the Halewijn cadet branches.''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Catharina (Catheline) van Diksmuide; Dame de Boesinghe (by marriage); Dowager of Saint-Omer-Wallon-Capelle'''
2 CONT
2 CONT (Generation 19 - Marital Alignment)
2 CONT '''I. VITAL SUMMARY'''
2 CONT Birth Name: Catharina (Catheline) van Dixmude / van Diksmuide
2 CONT Known Titles: Dame de Boesinghe (by marriage); Dowager of Saint-Omer-Wallon-Capelle
2 CONT Floruit (fl.) Dates: fl. mid-15th century – † 5 October 1483
2 CONT Burial: Parish Church of Boezinge (Boesinghe), West Flanders
2 CONT Status: High Noble Matriarch; Mother of the Halewijn-Boesinghe stem
2 CONT Parents: Oliver van Dixmude & [Mother Unrecorded/Unknown]
2 CONT Primary Residences: Estates of Wallon-Capelle (first marriage) and Kasteel van Boesinghe (second marriage)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''II. MARRIAGES AND ALIGNMENT'''
2 CONT Catharina's life was defined by two significant aristocratic unions that bridged the established coastal nobility with the expanding Burgundian-aligned houses.
2 CONT '''1. First Marriage: House of Saint-Omer'''
2 CONT She first married a lord of the illustrious House of Saint-Omer, explicitly noted in records as [N.] de Saint-Omer dit Wallon-Capelle. This union ended upon his death, leaving her a wealthy and well-connected widow in the regional
2 CONC  noble network.
2 CONT '''2. Second Marriage: House of Halewijn'''
2 CONT As her second husband, she married Jan V (Jean) dit 'Menen' van Halewijn, Heer van Rousselarie ende van Boesinghe († 14/18 April 1482). This marriage firmly established the Halewijn cadet line in the Yser valley and solidified the
2 CONC  lordship of Boesinghe.
2 CONT Children's Bloc (Issue with Jan V van Halewijn)
2 CONT Nicolaas (Nicolas / "Colard") van Halewijn: Primary son and heir; Seigneur de Boesinghe. Married Johanna van Stavele and continued the senior cadet line.
2 CONT Marguerite van Halewijn: Dowered into the regional Flemish nobility.
2 CONT (Note: Any issue from her first marriage to the Lord of Saint-Omer-Wallon-Capelle would belong to that specific patrimony and not the Halewijn succession).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''III. STRATEGIC ROLE AND KEY ACHIEVEMENTS'''
2 CONT While 15th-century record-keeping rarely attributes formal administrative titles to women outside of independent sovereign regencies, Catharina van Diksmuide played three distinct, high-impact strategic roles that directly dictate
2 CONC d the success and survival of the Halewijn-Boesinghe line:
2 CONT
2 CONT '''1. The Consolidation of Dual Dowry & Feudal Portions (The Financial Anchor)'''
2 CONT As the widow of a lord of Saint-Omer-Wallon-Capelle, Catharina entered her second marriage with Jan V van Halewijn carrying significant dower lands (douaire) and jointure rights from Artois/French Flanders. Combining her Saint-Ome
2 CONC r dower with her own inheritance as the daughter of Oliver van Dixmude gave Jan V the capital and territorial backing needed to split off from the main Halewijn trunk and establish a self-sustaining cadet seat at Boesinghe.
2 CONT Key Takeaway: Without the liquidity and territorial dower brought by Catharina’s first marriage and paternal lineage, Jan V—as a younger third son among 14 children—would have lacked the financial leverage to turn Boesinghe into a
2 CONC  primary dynastic seat.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''2. Territorial Unification of the Yser Corridor'''
2 CONT The Dixmude (Diksmuide) lineage held deep strategic rights along the Yser river valley. By marrying Catharina, Jan V achieved a critical geographic bridge between:
2 CONT The traditional Halewijn influence along the Leie (Menen/Roulers).
2 CONT The northern Yser/Ypres basin centered at Boezinge.
2 CONT Catharina served as the literal hereditary hinge that anchored the Halewijn family into the northern chatellany of Ypres, shielding their new domain via ancient regional kin networks.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''3. Securing the Boesinghe Patrimony for Generation 20'''
2 CONT Catharina’s ultimate dynastic achievement was ensuring the undisputed succession of her son, Nicolaas (Colard) van Halewijn. By outliving her husband by only 18 months, she managed the critical transition phase following Jan V's d
2 CONC eath in April 1482 during the chaotic political aftermath of Mary of Burgundy's death.
2 CONT She held the household and estate intact during this dangerous power vacuum, transferring a fully integrated, unencumbered estate to Nicolaas and securing his high-status marriage to Johanna van Stavele (of the powerful Lords of S
2 CONC tavele).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''IV. NARRATIVE BIOGRAPHY'''
2 CONT Born into the House of Diksmuide, Catharina (Catheline) was a daughter of Oliver van Dixmude, linking her to one of the most ancient and prominent noble families of the Flemish coastal plain. Women of her standing were critical no
2 CONC des in the intricate web of feudal alliances that held the Burgundian Netherlands together.
2 CONT
2 CONT Her early adulthood saw her married into a cadet branch of the powerful Artois family of Saint-Omer (the Wallon-Capelle line). Following her first husband's death, Catharina brought her aristocratic pedigree, dower rights, and reg
2 CONC ional influence into a second marriage with Jan V van Halewijn. Jan, a younger son of the vast Halewijn-Ghistelles household, was in the process of establishing his own seat of power.
2 CONT
2 CONT Together, Jan and Catharina anchored the Halewijn presence north of Ypres. While her husband managed the judicial and feudal administration of Roeselare and Boezinge, Catharina presided over the domestic and social governance of t
2 CONC he Boesinghe estate, raising the next generation of Halewijn lords—most notably their son Nicolaas, who would fully consolidate the Boesinghe patrimony.
2 CONT
2 CONT Catharina outlived her second husband by just over a year. After Jan V died in April 1482, Catharina passed away on 5 October 1483. They were buried together in the parish church of Boezinge, their lives commemorated by a joint ep
2 CONC itaph that stood as a physical testament to the founding of the Halewijn-Boesinghe dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''V. HERALDRY & ALLIANCE SHIELD'''
2 CONT * House of Diksmuide (Paternal): Or, a saltire gules (Gold shield with a red diagonal cross).
2 CONT * House of Halewijn (Second Marital): Argent, three lions rampant sable, armed, langued, and crowned or.
2 CONT * Alliance Shield: Impaled arms displaying Halewijn on the dexter (left side to the viewer) alongside the saltire of Diksmuide on the sinister (right side).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VI. DOCUMENTS AND EVIDENCE'''
2 CONT * Béthune, Baron Jean de: Epitaphes et monuments des églises et couvents de la Flandre... (1900/1898), p. 228. This is the definitive primary source for her death, transcribing the Boezinge epitaph: "Catheline van Dixmude" who die
2 CONC d 5 Oct 1483, alongside her husband "mer Jan van Halewijn ruddere...", with the manuscript explicitly noting her first marriage to "een van Sainct-Omer dict Wallon-capelle".
2 CONT * Cawley, Charles: Medieval Lands: Northern France - Bergues, Cassel, Béthune, Lille, Foundation for Medieval Genealogy (fmg.ac). Confirms her parentage (daughter of Oliver) and her dual marriages.
2 CONT * Pattou, Étienne: Maison de Halewijn (Hallewyn, Halluin), Racines et Histoire, detailing her as the wife of Jan and mother of the lords of Boezinge.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VII. CURATOR DISCLAIMER'''
2 CONT <span style="color:red; font-weight:bold;">DISCLAIMER: This profile represents True Generation 19 (son of Jan IV van Halewijn and Jacqueline de Ghistelles). Do not renumber this profile based on secondary French calendar indexes. 
2 CONC Critical Generational Resolution Note: Jan V is the father (mer Jans zuene) of Nicolaas "Colard" van Halewijn, Heer van Boesinghe. Do not conflate Jan V with his son Nicolaas or his grandson Jakob. Primary funerary evidence from t
2 CONC he church at Boezinge (transcribed by Béthune) explicitly confirms Jan V’s death on 14/18 April 1482 and his wife Catharina van Dixmude’s death on 5 October 1483. Verify all primary epitaphs and landbook entries against Flemish ca
2 CONC rtularies. Notice: AI-assisted biographical summary. Generative AI models can make mistakes, hallucinate details, or conflate generational data; readers are advised to check and verify against primary archival sources.</span>
