1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Maldeghem La Loyale by Mme la de Lalaing'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Elisabeth van Maldeghem, gehuwd met Joos van Halewijn, ridder, heer van Uitkerke, Buggenhout, Mesegem, Willecomme en Priems, raads- en kamerheer van Filips de Goede, grootbaljuw van Brugge en van het Vrije, zoon van Willem, heer v
2 CONC an Uitkerke, en van Margriete Bousin. De omstandigheden van dat huwelijk kwamen eerder al ter sprake. Ze kregen slechts één dochter, die jong stierf door een vreselijk ongeval. De juiste datum van Elisabeths overlijden is niet bek
2 CONC end, maar uit de registers van de schepenen van Gent blijkt dat ze op 8 november 1433 nog in leven was. Ze ligt begraven in de Sint-Pieterskerk te Maldegem*.
2 CONT Na de dood van zijn vrouw Elisabeth hertrouwde Joos van Halewijn met een jong meis­ je, Passchine Maerschalcx, en uit hen ontsproot de tak der Halewijns die heren werden van Maldegem. Joos van Halewijn overleed op 14 juni 1455 en 
2 CONC werd begraven in de augustijnenkerk te Brugge. Uit het tweede huwelijk werd Karel van Halewijn, heer van Uitkerke, geboren; hij huwde met Adrienne de Baenst en het is aan hun zoon dat Filips IX, heer van Maldegem, land en heerlijk
2 CONC heid van Maldegem heeft geschonken.
2 CONT
2 CONT English translation by DeepL: Elisabeth van Maldeghem, married to Joos van Halewijn, knight, lord of Uitkerke, Buggenhout, Mesegem, Willecomme and Priems, councilor and chamberlain of Philip the Good, grand bailiff of Bruges and o
2 CONC f the Free, son of Willem, lord of Uitkerke , and by Marguerite Bousin. The circumstances of that marriage have already been discussed. They had only one daughter, who died young in a terrible accident. The exact date of Elisabeth
2 CONC 's death is not known, but the registers of the aldermen of Ghent show that she was still alive on 8 November 1433. She is buried in the Sint-Pieterskerk in Maldegem*.
2 CONT After the death of his wife Elisabeth, Joos van Halewijn remarried a young girl, Passchine Maerschalcx, and from them sprang the branch of the Halewijns who became lords of Maldegem. Joos van Halewijn died on 14 June 1455 and was 
2 CONC buried in the Augustinian Church in Bruges. Karel van Halewijn, lord of Uitkerke, was born of the second marriage; he married Adrienne de Baenst and it is to their son that Philip IX, lord of Maldegem, gave land and lordship of Ma
2 CONC ldegem.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Martens, Marc, translator. “MALDEGHEM DIE LOYALE.” De Heemkundige Kring Het Ambacht Maldegem, Jaarboek 6, 26 Dec. 2000, p. 175-176''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The story behind the marriage of Joos van Halewijn and Elisabeth van Maldeghem, that neither wanted:'''
2 CONT …Willem van Halewijn, lord of Uitkerke, had first agreed to the marriage of his son Joos to the aforementioned Elisabeth van Maldeghem. The contract was drawn up on 17 August 1470 in the presence of witnesses, but when it had to b
2 CONC e signed, Willem van Halewijn refused. Very indignant about such a state of affairs that brought his family “très-grant blame, vitupère et deshonneur”, Geerard van Maldeghem saw no other way out than to turn to John, Duke of Burgu
2 CONC ndy and Count of Flanders. He presented the case to him and asked that justice be done. The duke insisted on first hearing both parties and summoned the parents and friends who had been present at the agreement. Then he ordered th
2 CONC at the marriage be concluded “sans aucun reffus ou difficulté” - without any objection or misunderstanding - on pain of Willem van Halewijn that all his goods would be placed under sequestration, after which the proceeds would com
2 CONC pensate Geerard van Maldeghem in proportion to the amounts stipulated in the compromise. The decision of the duke was issued on April 21, 1416.
2 CONT
2 CONT There was no turning back. So William of Halewijn had the marriage sealed that he cursed in the bottom of his heart. Poor Elisabeth had little reason to look forward to it with joy.
2 CONT From her union with Joos van Halewijn a little daughter was born, who met her end in the most horrible way: she fell into a kettle of boiling water and died a horrible death. Not long after, Joos had enough of the woman who had be
2 CONC en forced upon him so much that he did not hesitate to provide her with a rival. As you can see, Elisabeth did not have a happy life; it is said that she succumbed to her sorrow. Her husband then hastened to take his favorite as w
2 CONC ife, Passchine Maerschalcx. From that marriage was born Charles of Halewijn, lord of Uitkerke, about whom I have already spoken several times and in whose favour Philip IX of Maldeghem, shortly before his death, renounced the land
2 CONC s and seigneuries of Maldegem". Was I right to say that this marriage took place under the most gloomy auspices for our family? None of this detracts from the fact that the house of Halewijn is one of the most important families i
2 CONC n Flanders and, as Baron van Reiffenbergh states, one of the oldest and noblest families in the Netherlands.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''De Lalaing, Marie Henriette Octavie Ghislaine van Maldeghem, La Loyale: Memoires Et Archives Publiés Par Mme La De Lalaing née Comtesse De Maldeghem. Ve Wouters, 1849, p. 218-219''
