1 OCCU Seigneur de Beaurevoir et Richeburg, Lord of Beauvoir, Jean de Luxembourg, seigneur de Beaurevoir, Comte De Brienne, AKA "John of Luxembourg", Sire of Beauvois, Lord of Beavoir
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jean de Luxembourg, Seigneur de Beaurevoir et Richeburg
2 CONT
2 CONT M, #3497, b. circa 1370, d. circa 1397
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean de Luxembourg, Seigneur de Beaurevoir et Richeburg|b. c 1370\nd. c 1397|p350.htm#i3497|Guy de Luxembourg, Comte de Ligny|d. 22 Aug 1371|p351.htm#i3507|Maud de Châtillon|b. b 1350\nd. a 27 Aug 1371|p351.htm#i3508|||||||Jean de
2 CONC  Châtillon, Comte de St. Pol||p351.htm#i3509|Jeanne de Fiennes||p24884.htm#i248832|
2 CONT
2 CONT Last Edited=16 Jan 2003
2 CONT
2 CONT      Jean de Luxembourg, Seigneur de Beaurevoir et Richeburg was born circa 1370. He was the son of Guy de Luxembourg, Comte de Ligny and Maud de Châtillon. He married Margaret d'Enghein, daughter of Louis d'Enghein, Comte de Brie
2 CONC nne et Conversano and Joan de St. Severin, on 10 April 1384. He died circa 1397.
2 CONT
2 CONT      Jean de Luxembourg, Seigneur de Beaurevoir et Richeburg gained the title of Seigneur de Beaurevoir. He gained the title of Seigneur de Richeburg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Jean de Luxembourg, Seigneur de Beaurevoir et Richeburg and Margaret d'Enghein
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Pierre I de Luxembourg, Comte de St. Pol, Brienne et Conservan+ d. 31 Aug 1443
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite married her third husband, John of Luxembourg, Sire of Beauvois (1370- 1397). He was the son of Guy of Luxembourg, Count of Saint-Pol and Mahaut of Chatillon, Countess of Saint-Pol. By her third husband, Marguerite had 
2 CONC five children:[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter of Luxembourg (1390- 31 August 1433), Count of Saint-Pol (1430), which he inherited from his aunt Jeanne of Luxembourg, Countess of Saint-Pol and Ligny; he also inherited, on an unknown date, Marguerite's fiefs of Brienne an
2 CONC d of Conversano, thus becoming Count of Brienne and of Conversano. He married on 8 May 1405, Margaret de Baux, by whom he had nine children, including Jacquetta of Luxembourg, mother of Elizabeth Woodville Queen-Consort of Edward 
2 CONC IV of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT John II of Luxembourg, Count of Ligny (1392- 5 January 1441), inherited the title of Beauvois from his father, and the title of Ligny from his aunt, Jeanne of Luxembourg. On 23 November 1418, married Jeanne de Bethune, widow of Ro
2 CONC bert of Bar, Count of Marle and Soissons who had been killed at the Battle of Agincourt on 25 October 1415. John, who was an ally of the English during the Hundred Years War, received Joan of Arc as his prisoner, and subsequently 
2 CONC sold her to the English for 10,000 livres.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis of Luxembourg (died 18 September 1443). He was a statesman and a high-ranking churchman. His posts and clerical titles included Cardinal (1439), Archbishop of Rouen (1437), Chancellor of France (1425), Governor of Paris (143
2 CONC 6), Bishop of Thérouanne, Administrator of Ely (1437), Bishop of Frascati (1442). He was buried in Ely Cathedral.
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherne of Luxembourg (born c.1393)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne of Luxembourg (died 1420), married firstly, on 8 September 1415, Louis, Seigneur de Ghistelles (killed at the Battle of Agincourt); she married secondly on 28 October 1419, Jean IV, Viscount of Melun, Constable of Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT  Jean de Luxembourg, Lord of Beauvoir (John)   
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean was born in 1370 in Beauvoir, Picardy, France.  Jean's father was Guy de Luxembourg and his mother was Maud de Chatillon.  His paternal grandparents were Duke Jean de Luxembourg and Alix de Dampierre-Flandre, dame de Richebou
2 CONC rg; his maternal grandparents were Jean de Chatillon and Jeanne de Fiennes.  He was an only child.  He died at the age of 27 in 1397 in Luxembourg.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ancestor Pedigree Chart
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean's family with Margaret d'Enghein
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean and Margaret were married.  They had a son named Pierre.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children
2 CONT
2 CONT   Pierre de Luxembourg I, Seigneur de Beaurevoir et Richeburg
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierre was born about 1390 in Luxembourg.  He died on August 31st, 1433 in Chateau Rambures, Oisemont, France.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite married her third husband, John of Luxembourg, Sire of Beauvois (1370- 1397). He was the son of Guy of Luxembourg, Count of Saint-Pol and Mahaut of Chatillon, Countess of Saint-Pol. By her third husband, Marguerite had 
2 CONC five children:[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Peter of Luxembourg (1390- 31 August 1433), Count of Saint-Pol (1430), which he inherited from his aunt Jeanne of Luxembourg, Countess of Saint-Pol and Ligny; he also inherited, on an unknown date, Marguerite's fiefs of Brienne an
2 CONC d of Conversano, thus becoming Count of Brienne and of Conversano. He married on 8 May 1405, Margaret de Baux, by whom he had nine children, including Jacquetta of Luxembourg, mother of Elizabeth Woodville Queen-Consort of Edward 
2 CONC IV of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT John II of Luxembourg, Count of Ligny (1392- 5 January 1441), inherited the title of Beauvois from his father, and the title of Ligny from his aunt, Jeanne of Luxembourg. On 23 November 1418, married Jeanne de Bethune, widow of Ro
2 CONC bert of Bar, Count of Marle and Soissons who had been killed at the Battle of Agincourt on 25 October 1415. John, who was an ally of the English during the Hundred Years War, received Joan of Arc as his prisoner, and subsequently 
2 CONC sold her to the English for 10,000 livres.
2 CONT
2 CONT Louis of Luxembourg (died 18 September 1443). He was a statesman and a high-ranking churchman. His posts and clerical titles included Cardinal (1439), Archbishop of Rouen (1437), Chancellor of France (1425), Governor of Paris (143
2 CONC 6), Bishop of Thérouanne, Administrator of Ely (1437), Bishop of Frascati (1442). He was buried in Ely Cathedral.
2 CONT
2 CONT Catherne of Luxembourg (born c.1393)
2 CONT
2 CONT Jeanne of Luxembourg (died 1420), married firstly, on 8 September 1415, Louis, Seigneur de Ghistelles (killed at the Battle of Agincourt); she married secondly on 28 October 1419, Jean IV, Viscount of Melun, Constable of Flanders.
2 CONT
