2 CONT PERCY, Earl of NORTHUMBERLAND. 
2 CONT This right noble, and ancient family of Percy, is defcended from Mainfred de Percy, who came out of Denmark into Normandy, before the adventure of the famous Rollo there, and had iffue Geffery, that in the year 1190, took part wit
2 CONC h the faid Rollo in his expedition into that province, .... etc.
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_the-peerage-of-england-_collins-arthur_1756_4/page/84/mode/1up
2 CONT This couragious nobleman q married Elizabeth, eldeft daughter r to Edmund Mortimer, Earl of March, by Phillippa s his wife, only daughter and heir of Lionel Duke of Clarence, fecond furviving fon of King Edward the Third, by whom 
2 CONC he left iffue '''one fon, Henry''', and one daughter, Elizabeth; firft t married to John Lord Clifford, and afterwards to u Ralph Nevil, the fecond Earl of Weftmorland, of that family.
2 CONT On 8 October, 5 Hen. IV. the King ordered Elizabeth, wife of Sir Henry Percy, deceafed, to be arrefted , and brought before him w, to anfwer fuch queftions, as fhall be demanded of her by the faid King. But furthur of her I have n
2 CONC ot feen.
2 CONT Her fon '''Henry in his youth, was made a Knt. of the Bath x, at the coronation of King Henry the Fourth''', ....etc.
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_the-peerage-of-england-_collins-arthur_1756_4/page/91/mode/1up
2 CONT '''He left iffue by Eleanor his wife''', daughter k of Ralph Earl of Weftmorland, '''and widow of Richard Lord Spencer, nine fons, and two daughters, viz.''' Henry, who fucceeded him in his honours. 2. John, who died l in his chil
2 CONC dhood.
2 CONT Sir Thomas Percy, '''third fon''', in confideration of his fervices in the wars of France, and Scotland m, was created Lord Egremont, December 20, in 28 Hen. VI. ....etc. And valiantly fighting for King Henry the fixth, in the bat
2 CONC tle of Northampton, the 10th of July, 1460, was q flain in the King's tent, who was then taken prifoner. He was unmarried, fo that the title of Lord Egremont was entinct in him.
2 CONT Sir Ralph Percy, '''fourth fon''', was alfo an affertor of the Lancaftrian intereft, and loft his life in the battle at Heggelamore, in Northumberland, faying r, as he was faying r, as he was dying, ....etc. He died unmarried.
2 CONT John, and Henry t, '''fifth and fixth fons''', died young. William, '''feventh fon''', was Chancellor u of the univerfity of Cambridge, in 1451, ....etc.
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_the-peerage-of-england-_collins-arthur_1756_4/page/92/mode/1up
2 CONT He x died in 1462, 3 Edw. IV.
2 CONT Sir Richard Percy, '''eighth fon''', married y Catherine, daughter of Sir Henry Nevile, Knt. and widow of William Fairfax, of Walton, Efq; who died in 31 Hen. VI.; but I do not find he had iffue by her. George, ninth fon, was a Pr
2 CONC ebent in the collegiate church of Beverly.
2 CONT '''The two daughters were''' z, Catherine, wife of Edmund Grey, Earl of Kent; and Anne, firft married to Sir Thomas Hungerford, Knt. Lord Molins; fecondly, to Sir Laurence Rainsford, Knt.; and laftly, to Sir Hugh Vaughan, Knt.; fh
2 CONC e had iffue by the Lord Hungerford, on fole daughter and heir Mary, married to Edward Lord Haftings and Hungerford, father, by her, of George the firft Earl of Huntington, and brought a very great eftate into the family ; her moth
2 CONC er died July 5, 1522, and was buried in St. Michael's chapel, in St. Margaret's church, Weftminfter.
2 CONT I now return to Henry, the eldeft fon, Earl of Northumberland, who was 30 years of age, and upwards, when he fucceeded to the title.
2 CONT ....etc.
2 CONT He had to wife, before the 25th of Hen. VI. Eleanor, daughter and fole heir of Richard Poynings, who died in the life-
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_the-peerage-of-england-_collins-arthur_1756_4/page/93/mode/1up
2 CONT time of his father, Robert Lord Poynings, whose heir he alfo was; ....etc.
2 CONT https://archive.org/details/bim_eighteenth-century_the-peerage-of-england-_collins-arthur_1756_4/page/97/mode/1up
2 CONT ....etc.
2 CONT The faid Henry, Earl of Northumberland, had iffue by Eleanor his wife, beforementioned q, Henry, his fon and heir, and three daughters; Elizabeth, married to Henry Lord Scrope, of Bolton; Eleanor, to Reginald Weft, Lord De La Warr
2 CONC ; and Margaret, to Sir William Gafcoigne, of Gauthorpe, in Com, Ebor. Knt. who had iffue by her r a daughter Anne, married  to Sir Thomas Fairfax, of Walton, in Com. Ebor, anceftor, by her, to thofe of his name, Lords Vifcounts Fa
2 CONC irfax, of Emely. 
2 CONT Which Henry, being in his minority, when his father was flain, the King ordered him in cuftody in the Tower of London, till the 27th of October, 1469, 9 Edw. IV. s when he came before him in his palace at Weftminfter perfonally, a
