1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Philippe I de Croÿ, II. Count of Chimay=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Philip was the eldest son of Jean II de Croÿ, confidant of Philip the Good, and Mary of Lalaing was a noble from the House of Croÿ, in the service of the Dukes of Burgundy.
2 CONT * Philip I of Croÿ-Chimay, as Knight in the order of the Golden Fleece
2 CONT * Philip was knighted at the age of 17 after the Battle of Gavere. At a young age, he became grand baillif of Hainaut.[2] At the age of 28, he was appointed Chamberlain of Charles the Bold, who was not consulted in this decision. 
2 CONC This fact strengthened Charles's already strong hate towards the Croÿ family.
2 CONT * In 1477 Philip was taken prisoner in the Battle of Nancy, where Charles the Bold was killed. After his release, he entered in the service of Maximilian of Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Philip_I_of_Cro%C3%BF-Chimay Philip I of Croÿ-Chimay, by Wikipedia]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Philip I of Croÿ-Chimay''' (November 1436 – Bruges, September 14, 1482), count of Chimay, Lord of Quiévrain, was a noble from the House of Croÿ, in the service of the Dukes of Burgundy.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Life'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip was the eldest son of Jean II de Croÿ, confidant of Philip the Good, and Mary of Lalaing.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip was knighted at the age of 17 after the Battle of Gavere. At a young age, he became grand baillif of Hainaut. At the age of 28, he was appointed Chamberlain of Charles the Bold, who was not consulted in this decision. This 
2 CONC fact strengthened Charles's already strong hate towards the Croÿ family.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Charles the Bold came to power in 1465, he banished Philip, as well as his father Jean II and his uncle Antoine I de Croÿ. Philip was the first to reconcile with Charles in 1468.
2 CONT
2 CONT When his father died in 1473, Philip became the second count of Chimay. He became also a Knight in the Order of the Golden Fleece. Furthermore, he received the confiscated properties of Philippe de Commynes after his betrayal. Bet
2 CONC ween 1474 and 1477, Philip was Stadtholder of the newly conquered Duchy of Guelders.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1477 Philip was taken prisoner in the Battle of Nancy, where Charles the Bold was killed. After his release, he entered in the service of Maximilian of Austria.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Marriage and children=
2 CONT
2 CONT Philip married in 1453 with Walburga von Moers und Saarwerden. they had 5 children :
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Charles I of Croÿ-Chimay''' (1455–1527), count and later prince of Chimay
2 CONT * '''Antoine of Croÿ-Chimay''' (died 1546), lord of Sempy
2 CONT * '''Françoise,''' married Antoine of Luxembourg-Ligny, count of Charny
2 CONT * '''Catharina''', married Robert II de La Marck, Duke of Bouillon
2 CONT * '''Margareta''' (died 1514), married 1501 count Jacob III of Horne
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Links=
2 CONT
2 CONT * https://genealogics.org/getperson.php?personID=I00014096&tree=LEO
2 CONT
2 CONT =Sources=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Biographie Nationale, vol. 4, col. 563-564
2 CONT * Raphael de Smedt (ed.): Les chevaliers de l’ordre de la Toison d’or au XVe siècle. Notices bio-bibliographiques
2 CONT * Bietenholz, Peter G.; Deutscher, Thomas Brian, eds. (1995). Contemporaries of Erasmus: A Biographical Register of the Renaissance and Reformation. University of Toronto Press.
2 CONT * Jones, Michael (1986). Gentry and Lesser Nobility in Late Medieval Europe. Sutton.
2 CONT * Vaughan, Richard (2010). Philip the Good: The Apogee of Burgundy. Vol. 3. The Boydell Press.
2 CONT * Vaughan, Richard (2002). Charles the Bold: The Last Valois Duke of Burgundy. The Boydell Press.
2 CONT * Paravicini, Werner (2020). Montée, crise, réorientation. Pour unse histoire de la famille de Croy au XVe siècle, in: Revue belge de philologie et d'histoire 98, 2, pp. 149–355.
