1 OCCU 2nd count of Lalaing, baron of Escornaix and Montigny, chevalier de Toison d’Or, etc.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Notes
2 CONT Il fut d'abord destiné à devenir ecclesiastique comme cadet. Il fut prieur commandataire de Saint Sauve près de Valenciennes vers 1519. Il fut évêque de Coria en Espagne. Après la mort de ses deux frères ainés, en 1524, il prit l'
2 CONC épée et se maria. Chevalier de la Toison d'or, grand bailli de Hainaut, gouverneur général des Pays-Bas en 1557. Mort de la dysentrie.
2 CONT Available Sources : Chanoine de Joigny, 1935, L de Herckenrode, 1868, F Brassart, 1879, FV Goethals, 1862, de Saint-Genois, 1781, C Sars de Solmont, 1846.
2 CONT Death
2 CONT de Herckenrode donne le 22 novembre comme date du décès.
2 CONT Birth: 
2 CONT - L de Herckenrode, 1868.
2 CONT - F Brasssart, 1879.
2 CONT Spouse 1, Spouse 2: F Brasssart, 1879.
2 CONT Death: 
2 CONT - L de Herckenrode, 1868.
2 CONT - F Brasssart, 1879.
2 CONT - FV Goethals, 1862.
2 CONT _____
2 CONT '''Charles II de Lalaing''' ° ~1506 + 21/11/1558 (Brussels, of dysentery)
2 CONT (inh. in Lalaing) Prior Commandant of Saint-Sauve (~1519), 
2 CONT - Bishop of Coria (Spain, 1520-1524), resigns his benefices to marry (1524 after the death of his brother), 
2 CONT - 2nd count of Lalaing, 
2 CONT - baron of Escornaix and Montigny, 
2 CONT - lord of Bracle, Saint-Aubin and Wavrin, 
2 CONT - knight of the Golden Fleece (183°, 1531, Tournai), 
2 CONT - captain of a band of Ordinance, 
2 CONT - Chamberlain & adviser of Emperor Charles V & King Philip II, 
2 CONT - Grand Bailiff, 
2 CONT - Governor of Utrecht then of the Duchy of Luxembourg & County of Chiny, 
2 CONT - Governor-General of the Netherlands (1530-1531 & 1557-1588), 
2 CONT - Grand-Bailiff, Governor & Captain-General of Hainaut, Cambrai & Cambrésis (04/1549-1556, - following the death of Duke Philippe d'Arschot), 
2 CONT - State Councillor (1540), 
2 CONT - Chief of Finances (1544), X 1543 (Sittart, at the head of the light cavalry), 
2 CONT - Ambassador to England (1553, marriage proposal of Queen Marie Tudor for Philippe of Spain), 
2 CONT - Ambassador to Admiral de Coligny, concluded the Peace of Vaucelles (near Cambrai, 05/02/1556: 5-year truce broken 1557), 
2 CONT X 1557 (Saint-Quentin)
2 CONT
2 CONT married 1) 30/08/1528 (Binche) Marguerite de Croÿ-Chimay, lady of Wavrin and Escaussines, Seneschal of Flanders ° 06/12/1508
2 CONT + 11/07/1540 (or 1549 ?) (youngest daughter of Charles, Prince of Chimay & of the Holy Empire, baron of Quiévrain, lord of Escaussines, knight of the Golden Fleece, and of Louise d'Albret, lady of Avesnes, Landrecies, Saint-Venant
2 CONC , etc.) > 12 children
2 CONT
2 CONT ép. 2) ~1550 Marie de Montmorency-Hornes + 05/02/1570
2 CONT (eldest daughter of Joseph de Montmorency, lord of Nevele,
2 CONT and Anne d'Egmont-Bureneveuve; sister of the count of Hornes; married 2) 1562 Pierre Ernest, count of Mansfelt, knight of the Golden Fleece, Governor of Luxembourg) (she acquired the seigneury of Condé) > 4 children
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Pattou, Etienne. “Famille & Seigneurs De Lalaing.” Racines et Histoire''
2 CONT __________
2 CONT '''CHARLES II de Lalaing'''
2 CONT count of Lalaing, knight of the Golden Fleece in 1531, governor and grand bailiff of Hainaut, governor general of the Netherlands in 1557, captain of a band of ordinance, adviser of state, chief of finances; having been employed b
2 CONC y Charles V and Philip II "in their greatest and most important affairs, as much of peace as of war. Born about 1506, he died of dysentery, in Brussels, on November 21, 1558 (76), holding then the first rank at the court, because 
2 CONC of his age, his name and the great affairs which he had treated for a long time for his masters; but he had made an enemy of the famous Granvelle, favorite minister of Philip II, in the spirit of which the future prince of the Chu
2 CONC rch had already lost his adversary with his ordinary skill.
2 CONT Of the magnificent tomb, which was erected in the church of Lalaing, our Museum preserves the reclining statue and the inscription on a large copper plate.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had first been destined for ecclesiastical honors, as a cadet; commendatory prior of Saint-Sauve near Valenciennes around 1519, the coadjutorship of the bishopric of Utrecht and the hat of cardinal were in 1522 solicited for hi
2 CONC m by Charles V from Pope Adrian VI, in consideration of his uncle, the all-powerful Count of Hoochstrate (77). He was bishop of Coria in Spain. It was not before 1524 (although his elder brother had died since 1521) that he resign
2 CONC ed his benefices to take up the sword and marry.
2 CONT
2 CONT From his first wife, Marguerite DE CROY-Chimay, who died in 1549, and whom he had married in Binche on August 30, 1528, he had :
2 CONT
2 CONT 1° Philippe, who follows.
2 CONT
2 CONT 2° to 12° Eleven other children who died young, before their father. 
2 CONT
2 CONT From his second wife, Marie DE MONTMORENCY (sister of the famous Earl of Hornes), whom he married around 1550 and who married, in 1562, Count Pierre Ernest de Mansfelt (78), knight of the Fleece, governor of Luxembourg, he had at 
2 CONC least four children:
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000189077410870&size=large}}
2 CONT Château de Bailleul in Condé where Marie de Montmorency and Charles Comte de Lalaing lived and where the Princess of Espinoy was born by YVES DUCOURTIOUX
2 CONT
2 CONT Correspond, of Charles V and Adrian VI , Bruxell, 1839, in-8°, pp, XXXIV, XXXVII and 204.
2 CONT
2 CONT 13° Hugues de Lalaing lord of Condé, born in Valenciennes in November 1551, baptized on the 23rd in the church of Saint-Jean, with great solemnity, having for godparents Hugues de Meteun, prince of Espinoy, and the lord of Bugnico
2 CONC urt (Pontus de Lalaing), and for godmother his grandmother Anne d'Egmont, Dowager Countess of Hornes. In 1565 he was described as a "young lord of great luster, quality and reputation," which did not prevent him from becoming "deb
2 CONC ilitated in spirit" (from before 1580), a state in which he lived until 1618; he lies in the church of Notre-Dame de Condé. In 1583, Pontus Heuterus, in his Genealogiae, page 47, referred to him as vir non integrae mentis.
2 CONT
2 CONT 14° Charles de Lalaing, born in Valenciennes on March 4, 1553 (old style), baptized on the 15th, in the collegiate church of Salle-le-Comte, by the abbot of Saint-Jean, "with great pomp and magnificence," having for godparents Cha
2 CONC rles V, represented by the count of Boussu, and Mr. de Wimes, and for godmother the regent of the Netherlands, Marie, sister of the emperor, represented by the duchess of Arschot. He died young, before his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT 15° Emmanuel de Lalaing, lord of Montigny (Saint-Christophe), known as the baronde Montigny, marquis of Renty (by his wife), knight of the golden Touson in 1586, governor and grand bailli of Hainaut in 1582, admiral of the Belgian
2 CONC  sea, general of the Walloon infantry, captain of a band of ordinance; born in Valenciennes in 1557, died in Mons on December 27, 1590, husband of Anne de CROY-Renty. He lies in the heart of the church of Condé.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the troubles, he stood out as prince of the Malcontents, having then the situation, almost without example, of a twenty-one year old general who did not obey any of the powers that were fighting over a country given over to
2 CONC  civil war and invasion, and who was strong enough to hold the destiny of his country in his hands. He ended up declaring himself for the cause of Spain, a resolution that was far from being disinterested. We know these children o
2 CONC f his:
2 CONT
2 CONT ..A. Alexandre de Lalaing, known as the Marquis de Renty, born in Valenciennes around November 1583, killed in the rescue of the besieged L'Ecluse, in 1604.  
2 CONT
2 CONT ..B. Marguerite de Lalaing , born in Valenciennes, December 1584 , died before her father.
2 CONT
2 CONT ..C. Jeanne de Lalaing, lady of Condé, married to Jean DE CROY, count of Solre, knight of the Golden Fleece, "the most gallant and the most, big spender of the Low Countries in his time", according to Vuoerden (78). Died in 1649. 
2 CONC Of which posterity, notably the modern dukes of Croy.
2 CONT
2 CONT 16° Philippe-Chrétienne de Lalaing, married by contract of April 26, 1571, passed in Luxembourg, with. Pierre DE MELEUN, then called: "Pierre de Werchin, seneschal heritable de Haynaut"; the duke of Alba was represented by the adv
2 CONC iser of Assonleville (79). Her husband, having taken back in 1579 the name and arms of Meleun, after the death of his elder brother, became famous under the title of prince of Espinoy. The heroine of Tournai died in Antwerp on 8 J
2 CONC une 1582 (80). She "was buried in the choir of the church of the abbey Sainct-Michel in the said city, according to the Roman ceremonies". (81)
2 CONT
2 CONT She left no posterity. In October 1580, she gave birth to a son in Tournai (who died young). Here is what one reads in a letter of the prince of Espinoy to the famous prince of Orange: "... You have been kind enough to thank me hu
2 CONC mbly for the congratulations you have given me, that God has been pleased to give me a son in my greatest adversity in such a turbulent time. I hope that he will believe in all virtue, to, with time, hold the same part of his fath
2 CONC er and return to his country .... Madam, my mother made me this honor to raise my son of the bottom of baptism, and maina in its compaingnie madam de Lallaing wife of the count Philippe; to see hereafter, and my ladies my sisters:
2 CONC  they did not forget anything to me well to prescribe and ramente to see my salvation... ". (82). It is known that the prince of Espinoy, of his goods by the Spaniards, was obstinate, although Catholic, in the fight against the fo
2 CONC reign domination.
2 CONT
2 CONT (76) Nicolas Le Borgne, élu d'Artois, composa un epicedium ou poême funèbre en l'honneur dit défunt (Ferry de Locres, Chronicon, Arras, 1616, in-4, p. 692).
2 CONT (77) Correspond, de Charles-Quint et d'Adrien VI , Bruxell, 1839, in-8°, pp, XXXIV, XXXVII et 204.
2 CONT (78) Compte rendu des séances de la Com. royale d'histoire, Bruxelles, 1869, in-8o, 3e série, X, p. 338.
2 CONT (79) Archives du parlement de Flandres, au greffe de la cour d'appel de Douai, fonds du greffe de Malines, sac no 180; copie codationnée.
2 CONT (80) « Estant sortye, après la rendition de Tournay, pour aler trouuar son mary en Angers, morut en chemin, estant tombée » la repuers »; f° 478 du Ms.fr. 744 de la bibl. nationale; Nécrologe de Frauchomme, composé vers 1620 à Hou
2 CONC plmes sur la Lys. — L'exactitude de l'anecdote est d'autant plus douteuse que la princesse, qui quitta Tournai le 1er décembre 1581, vécut encore pendant six mois,
2 CONT (81) Le Petit, L'Histoire des Pays-Bas, à St-Gervais, 1604, pet. in-8°, Il, p.52.
2 CONT (82) Gachard, Correspond de Guillamme le Taciturne, IV, p. 257.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Brassart, Felix, Société Académique, editor. Souvenirs De La Flandre Wallonne, Catalogue des Nobels de Nom, de Lalaing, Dix-Septieme, L. Crépin, 1882. un Comite Historique et Archeologique: Memories of Walloon Flanders: pp. 68-7
2 CONC 3.''
2 CONT __________
2 CONT '''CHARLES de Lalaing''', son of CHARLES Comte de Lalaing & his wife Jacqueline de Luxembourg ([1506]-Brussels 21 Nov 1558, bur Lalaing).  Comte de Lalaing.  An epitaph at Lalaing records the burial of “Charles comte de Lalaing do
2 CONC yen des pairs de Hainau, sire du pays d’Escornaix, baron de Condé, de Montigny, de Wavrin, seneschal de Flandre, seigneur de Bracle, Wazieres, St-Aubin, Escaussines” who married firstly “Marguerite de Croy, dame de Wavrin et d’Esc
2 CONC aussines, fille au prinice de Chimay” who died 2 Jul 1540 leaving one surviving child “Philippe comte de Lalain” from the 12 children born during their 22 years of marriage, and secondly “Marie de Montmorency” from whom he had son
2 CONC s and one daughter, died in Brussels 21 Nov 1558 aged 52 years, and includes other biographical details[550]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m firstly (Binche 30 Aug 1528) MARGUERITE de Croy Dame de Wavrin, d’Escaussines, de Marpon et de Berbières, daughter of CHARLES de Croy Prince de Chimay & his wife Louise d’Albret (6 Dec 1508-2 Jul 1540).  Père Anselme records tha
2 CONC t Philippe Seigneur de Wavrin mortgaged his lands to Charles de Croÿ Prince de Chimay, who granted it to his daughter Marguerite on her marriage to Charles Comte de Lalaing[551].  An epitaph at Lalaing records the burial of “Charl
2 CONC es comte de Lalaing...” who married firstly “Marguerite de Croy, dame de Wavrin et d’Escaussines, fille au prinice de Chimay” who died 2 Jul 1540 leaving one surviving child “Philippe comte de Lalain” from the 12 children born dur
2 CONC ing their 22 years of marriage[552]. 
2 CONT
2 CONT m secondly ([1550]) MARIE de Montmorency, daughter of JOSEPH de Montmorency Heer van Nevele & his wife Anna van Egmond.  An epitaph at Lalaing records the burial of “Charles comte de Lalaing...” who married secondly “Marie de Mont
2 CONC morency” from whom he had sons and one daughter[553].  She married secondly (1562) as his second wife, Peter Ernst Graf von Mansfeld. 
2 CONT Charles & his first wife had children: 
2 CONT 1.         '''PHILIPPE de Lalaing''' (Valenciennes 1553-Valenciennes 24 May 1582, bur Lalaing).  An epitaph at Lalaing records the burial of “Charles comte de Lalaing...” who married firstly “Marguerite de Croy, dame de Wavrin et 
2 CONC d’Escaussines, fille au prinice de Chimay” who died 2 Jul 1540 leaving one surviving child “Philippe comte de Lalain” from the 12 children born during their 22 years of marriage[554].  Comte de Lalaing.  m (1569) MARGUERITE de Lig
2 CONC ne d’Arenberg, daughter of JEAN de Ligne Seigneur de Barbançon & his wife Marguerite de la Marck Ctss d’Arenberg (-24 Feb 1611, bur Lalaing).  Philippe & his wife had children:   
2 CONT a)         MARGUERITE de Lalaing (Lalaing [Apr] 1574-21 Feb 1650, Brussels, Berlaimont monastery).  Ctss de Lalaing.  m ([Sep 1592]) as his second wife, FLORENT Comte de Berlaimont, son of --- (-1620). 
2 CONT b)         FRANÇOIS de Lalaing (Mons 1577-Douai 11 Feb 1590, bur Lalaing).  Comte de Lalaing. 
2 CONT c)         CHRISTINE de Lalaing .  m MAXIMILIEN Comte de Bailleul, son of ---. 
2 CONT Charles & his second wife had children: 
2 CONT 2.         '''EMMANUEL PHILIBERT de Lalaing''' (Valenciennes 5 May 1557-Mons 27 Dec 1590, bur Condé Notre-Dame).  Baron de Montigny.  An epitaph at Condé Notre-Dame records the burial of “Emmanuel de Lalaing...marchioni de Renty, 
2 CONC bar. de Montigny, D. de Condé...” who died 27 Dec 1590 aged 33[555].  m as her first husband, ANNE de Croy Marquise de Renty et de Chèvres, daughter of GUILLAUME de Croÿ Seigneur de Renty & his wife Anne de Renesse.  She married s
2 CONC econdly as his second wife, Philippe de Croÿ Comte de Solre.  Emmanuel Philibert & his wife had children: 
2 CONT a)         JEANNE de Lalaing (-1649).  Dame de Renty.  m (1608) JEAN de Croy Comte de Solre, son of PHILIPPE de Croÿ Comte de Solre & his first wife Anne de Beaufort Dame de Beaufort-en-Artois et de Ranssart (-Madrid 1640). 
2 CONT b)         ALEXANDRE de Lalaing (Valenciennes Nov 1583-killed near Lécluse 1604).  Marquis de Renty. 
2 CONT 3.         '''PHILIPPOTTE CHRETIENNE de Lalaing''' (-Antwerp 8 Jun 1582, bur Antwerp St Michel).  m (contract 26 Apr 1571) as his first wife, PIERRE de Melun, son of HUGUES de Melun Prince d’Epinoy & his wife Yolande de Barbançon 
2 CONC Dame de Roubais (-1594).  Prince d’Epinoy, Baron d’Antoing 1579. 
2 CONT
