1 NOTE {geni:about_me} The confusion in many records stems from the fact that Charles I had two sons named Philippe—one from each marriage—and they both cycled through the "Escornaix" designation. Using Curator Logic, we can dismantle th
2 CONC is "Double Philippe" problem by looking at their mothers and their ultimate marriages.
2 CONT
2 CONT II. Philippe #2: The Baron (Cadet Line)
2 CONT Branch: Cadet Line (Second Marriage)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mother: Jacqueline de Luxembourg-Fiennes
2 CONT
2 CONT This Philippe took over the "Escornaix" identity permanently. He represents the Luxembourg-Lalaing connection.
2 CONT
2 CONT Primary Title: Baron d'Escornaix (Schorisse).
2 CONT
2 CONT Wife: Florence de Wied-Runkel.
2 CONT
2 CONT Key Asset: His prestige was derived from his mother's Luxembourg blood. He focused on the administrative and territorial management of the Escornaix and Wavrin estates.
2 CONT
2 CONT Status: He did not attain the Golden Fleece, distinguishing him from his more famous half-brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Title Shift: When his older half-brother (Philippe #1) moved up to the Hoogstraten title, the "Sieur d’Escornaix" designation became available as an appanage for the second family.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Status: He remained Baron d'Escornaix. He never attained the Comtal (Count) rank of his brother.
2 CONT
2 CONT Key Marker: Marriage to Florence de Wied. This branch remained focused on the Flemish-Imperial borderlands and did not reach the same "Stadtholder" level of power as the senior Philippe.
