2 _MARNM de Lalaing
1 NAME Philippe //
2 GIVN Philippe
2 SURN de Lalaing
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1503
1 DEAT
2 DATE BET 11 APR 1550 AND 1563
2 ADDR
3 CITY Utrecht
3 STAE Utrecht
3 CTRY Netherlands
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Notes
2 CONT Fils naturel, légitimé en mars 1523. 
2 CONT -Chevalier, 
2 CONT - seigneur de Mouillerie (Motterie) et de Mafle, 
2 CONT - maître d'hotel de Marguerite d'Autriche, 
2 CONT - régente des Pays-Bas. 
2 CONT - Commissaire au renouvellement des magistrats de Flandres (1528, 1529, 1535, 1536, 1538). 
2 CONT - Ambassadeur en France pour l'Empereur Charles Quint.
2 CONT
2 CONT Un Philippe de Lalaing, seigneur de Mouillerie et Maffle et décédé le 11 avril 1550 est qualfié de bâtard. Il s'agit peut-être du même.
2 CONT Hier legt begraven den erentfesten ende vromen Philips, basterd van Lalain, ridder, heer van Mouillerie, Mafle etc., starf anno XVc ende L, den XI april.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources: A van Buchel, 1648, L de Herckenrode, 1868, Ed Dhont - De Waepenaert, 1871, J Le Roux, 1715, FV Goethals, 1862, FV Goethals, 1850, R Goffin, 1974.
2 CONT
2 CONT On trouve dans les Poorterboeken van Oudenaarde : Phelips (bastaert) de lalaing fs Mer Anthuenis, ruddere vanden Oerdene der K.M., heere van Hoochstraete etc., vrij poorter by letter der voorseide K.M. actum den xij-en dach van au
2 CONC gste anno duust Vc XXvij (12 août 1527). Cette bourgeoisie reçue par faveur royale le 12 août 1527, soit le jour de son mariage avec Florence de Rechem lui a sans doute permis d'éviter d'avoir à payer une taxe correspondant à 10% 
2 CONC des biens de son épouse. Sa mère pourrait avoir été Marie d'Autriche, reine-douairière de Hongrie, soeur de Charles-Quint.
2 CONT Selon Goethals (1862), sa mère était Florence de Rechem.
2 CONT Sources: Gilliodts-van Severen, 1867, Le Roux, 1784, de Vegiano, 1779, J Gailliard, 1858, L van Lerberghe et ! Ronsse, 1845, de Saint Genois, et al., 1848, Dumont, 1775, C Sars de Solmont, 1846.
2 CONT Spouse: 
2 CONT - Ed Dhont - De Waepenaert, 1871
2 CONT - J de Saint Genois, et al., 184
2 CONT Death: A van Buchel, 1648.
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippe de Lalaing (~1500-1550) chevalier (après 1538) seigneur de La Mouillerie et de Maffle, Bourgeois d’Audenarde, écuyer & maître d’hôtel (1526) de l’Archiduchesse Marguerite d’Autriche (duchesse de Savoie, Régente des Pays-B
2 CONC as), Ambassadeur Extraordinaire en France pour Charles Quint (1530) attaché à la Maison de son parent Philippe de Lalaing, comte d’Hoogstraeten (brise ses armes de Lalaing-Hoogstraeten d’un filet d’or posé en barre) 
2 CONT
2 CONT ép. 1527 (Audenarde) Florence de Rechem, dame d’un fief appelé « vicomté d’Audenarde», mouvant de la Cour du Perron d’Audenarde, dame de Kerchove, Bolancy et Callenberghe + ~1587 (ou 1592 ?) (fille aînée de Josse, vicomte d’Audena
2 CONC rde, seigneur de Kerckhove et Calberghe, Lieutenant & Haut-Bailli du Pays d’Alost, et de Barbe Stommelincx)
2 CONT
2 CONT Philippe de Lalaing (~1500-1550) knight (after 1538) lord of La Mouillerie and Maffle, burgher of Audenarde, squire & butler (1526) of Archduchess Marguerite of Austria (duchess of Savoy, Regent of the Netherlands), Ambassador Ext
2 CONC raordinary in France for Charles V (1530) attached to the House of his relative Philippe de Lalaing, count of Hoogstraeten (breaks his arms of Lalaing-Hoogstraeten of a golden net in bar) 
2 CONT
2 CONT married in 1527 (Oudenaarde) Florence de Rechem, lady of a fief called "Viscounty of Oudenaarde", belonging to the Court of the Perron of Oudenaarde, lady of Kerchove, Bolancy and Callenberghe + ~1587 (or 1592 ?) (eldest daughter 
2 CONC of Josse, Viscount of Oudenaarde, lord of Kerckhove and Calberghe, Lieutenant & High bailiff of the Land of Alost, and of Barbe Stommelincx)
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000189496032891&size=large}}
2 CONT Philip van Lalaing à cheval 1543, by Cornelis Anthonisz
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000189496400863&size=large}}
2 CONT Philip de Lalaing, Count of Hoogstraten, by Hans Liefrinck I (before 1550)
2 CONT
2 CONT Curator note: the above images show the sequence of Philippe's coats of arms.  In the first c. 1543 he bore the basic Lalaing, seigneurs de Lalaing, in the second c. 1550 he bore the Lalaing counts of Hoogstraeten as indicated by 
2 CONC the lion rampant gules in the first lozenge.  this sequence would be consistent with history as his father died C. 1540 and the first image was drawn previous to 1540 vice 1543.  Also, despite numerous sources indicating that Phil
2 CONC ippe was a bastard, including a letter of legitimacy by Charles Quint, the only source that indicates this in the coat of arms is Brassart, who speaks o that image shown as the profile image with the gold bend from dexter to sinis
2 CONC ter indicating bastardy. 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Philippe, Bâtard de Lalaing''' ° ~1500 (1503 ?) + 30/06/1555
2 CONT (légitimé par Lettres de l’Empereur Charles-Quint à Malines en 03/1524 ns, moyennant finance de 60 £ ; acte amplié à Bruxelles le 23/03/1535 ns
2 CONT & enregistré à Lille le 08/04/1535
2 CONT - chevalier (après 1538) 
2 CONT - seigneur de La Mouillerie et de Maffle, Bourgeois d’Audenarde, 
2 CONT - écuyer & maître d’hôtel (1526) de l’Archiduchesse Marguerite d’Autriche (duchesse de Savoie, Régente des Pays-Bas), 
2 CONT - Ambassadeur Extraordinaire en France pour Charles-Quint (1530)
2 CONT - attaché à la Maison de son parent Philippe de Lalaing, 
2 CONT - comte d’Hoogstraeten (brise ses armes de Lalaing-Hoogstraeten d’un filet d’or posé en barre)
2 CONT
2 CONT ép. 12/08/1527 (Audenarde) Florence van/de Rechem, dame d’un fief appelé «vicomté d’Audenarde», mouvant de la Cour du Perron d’Audenarde, 
2 CONT - dame de Kerchove, Bolancy et Callenberghe + 1592 (fille aînée de Josse, vicomte d’Audenarde, seigneur de Kerckhove et Calberghe, Lieutenant & Haut-Bailli du Pays d’Alost,et de Barbe Stommelincx)
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Pattou, Etienne. “Famille & Seigneurs De Lalaing.” Racines et Histoire''
2 CONT __________
2 CONT B. '''Philippe de Lalaing''', born around 1500, legitimized by letters dated of Malines, in March 1523, by means of finance, taxed at sixty livres, at the time of the recording in Lille (20th register, f° vijxxX v°), having "been 
2 CONC engendered with the body of a damoiselle Ysabeau, bâtarde of Haubourdin, icelle estant constituted in estat de mariage, which is a condition notably aggravating the said fault of progeniture, how much, on the day of the nativity o
2 CONC f the said Philippe, she feusse devenue vesve", as it is explained in the "ampliation" dated of Brussels, March 23, 1534 (old style), and registered in Lille, April 8, 1535 (22nd register, f° cjx v°).
2 CONT
2 CONT According to the genealogical table of the cabinet of the titles, the count of Hoochstrate would have had his bastards Philippe and Antoine of the same woman" damoiselle Isabeau de Luxembourg, bde de Haubourdin, concubine," which 
2 CONC carried: Argent with the lion of Gules, the tail forked passed in saltire, crowned, lampassed and armed of gold, with the net broaching of azure posed in bar.
2 CONT
2 CONT This bastard of Haubourdin was certainly the daughter of Jean de Luxembourg, bastard of Saint-Pol, knight, lord of Haubourdin, son of the too famous constable of Saint-Pol, and of Amele de Scuere, "at the time they were both marri
2 CONC ed," legitimized, in 1498, being "already an anchor man" (70), died in Lille in 1503. In an enumeration of the fief of the castle of "Amerin" served to the countess of Vendôme, in 1508, by lady Anne Carondelet, widow of the aforem
2 CONC entioned lord of Haubourdin, it is spoken about four smallholdings of ground "belonging to Miss Ysabeau de Habourdin, which are located and enclosed with the perimeter of the aforementioned castle, which she holds of the aforement
2 CONC ioned castle of Amerin" (71).
2 CONT
2 CONT Old genealogical manuscripts designate the mother of Philippe de Lalaing as follows: "Isabeau de Luxembourg, bastard daughter of Ferdinand (sic) de Luxembourg, lord of Hautbourdin, and of Jacqueline de Bruaen" (72). Philippe de La
2 CONC laing is the author of the branch of Audenarde, which will follow. According to the genealogy of Lalaing of the collection Malotau, the count Antoine would have had, of the "particular damsel" who had been mother of his bastard An
2 CONC toine, a girl: Hélène de Lalaing, married to Melchior, bastard of Culembourg, of which she had no child, "then made religious and became abbess of..." (73).
2 CONT
2 CONT In the Correspondence of Maximilian and Margaret of Austria, published by the archivist Le Glay (Paris, Renouard, 1839, in-8°, I, page 302), there is a letter from the emperor to his daughter, dated Veylheym, July 24, 1510 concern
2 CONC ing "Anthoine de Lalaing, seigneur de Montygny," who wished to resign the office of bailli de Lens in Artois, "au prouffit de Charles de Bugnicourt (sic), son beau filz. " Although there is no misprint, as we have noted by checkin
2 CONC g the original document, nevertheless this passage of the imperial dispatch is not more understandable; it is about Charles de Bernemicourt, esquire, lord of La Thieuloye, who was indeed bailiff of Lens; but how could he be the so
2 CONC n-in-law of Antoine de Lalaing, who was not married then? and this expression cannot be understood in the sense of son-in-law (of the left hand), whereas: the lord of Montigny was too young to have already, in 1510, a married bast
2 CONC ard; finally according to the genealogies, the wife of Charles de Bernemicourt was called Florence Le Fevre dit de Hemstede, heiress of Liefvelt in Holland (74). Obviously the Imperial Chancellery will have made a mistake in writi
2 CONC ng "his beautiful son", as it had just made one in writing "Bugnicourt".
2 CONT
2 CONT (70) Archives départ., chambre des comptes, 17e reg, des chartes. f° lix v°. Cette légitimation est omise en la table du registre.
2 CONT (71) nation., collection Moreau, vol. 260, f° 134; tiré des archives du château d'Havrincourt.
2 CONT (72) Goethals, Diction, généalog. et herald., Bruxelles, 1850, in-4, III, sans pagination : Lalaing.
2 CONT (73) Bibl. publique de Douai, Ms. 891, vol. 10, f° 193.
2 CONT (74) Laurent Le Blond, Quartiers gériéalog., Bruxelles, 1788, in-8, I, p. 146. — Les portraits de « Charles de Bernemcourt, sr de La Thieulloye», et de sa femme « Flurence de Hemstere» figurent, sous les nos 247 et 248, dans le pr
2 CONC écieux recueil de la Bibliothèque d'Arras , Ms. 266 du Catalogue Caron),Cf. Dinaux, Arch. hist. et littér., Valenciennes, 1852, in 8, 3e série. III, p, 186. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Brassart, Felix, Société Académique, editor. Souvenirs De La Flandre Wallonne, Catalogue des Nobels de Nom, de Lalaing, Dix-Septieme, L. Crépin, 1882. un Comite Historique et Archeologique: Memories of Walloon Flanders: pp. 62-6
2 CONC 5''.
2 CONT __________
2 CONT Historiek
2 CONT
2 CONT Voormalig herenhuis gebouwd op de plaats van het oude pandjeshuis of Lombardenhuis van Pamele (tot eind 15de eeuw?). In 1519 vermeld als "Hof van Schorisse", woning van stadsgouverneur Charles I de Lalaing, heer van Schorisse en z
2 CONC ijn echtgenote Jacqueline van Gavere, weduwe van Jan van Luxemburg. Later zogenaamd "Huis van Luxemburg", bewoond door de zoon van bovengenoemde, Philippe de Lalaing. Ook zogenaamd "Hotel van Egmont", confer akte van 1714, naar de
2 CONC  bewoners in het vierde kwart van de 16de eeuw, Guillaume de Lalaing en zijn echtgenote Marie Christine van Egmont, dochter van graaf Lamoraal van Egmont. Ten slotte ook zogenaamd "Hotel de Berlaimont", naar de dochter van Philipp
2 CONC e de Lalaing, Marguerite, gehuwd met Florent, graaf van Berlaimont. Eind 17de eeuw werd het goed eigendom van de familie de Kerckhove, heren van Etikhove en Ladeuze. Achtereenvolgende verkopingen; onder meer in 1868 aan de familie
2 CONC  Boulez, die het Oudenaards tapijtwerk waarmee de salons waren bekleed verkochten aan het Engelse vorstenhuis; in 1919 aan chirurg De Meulemeester, herstel oorlogsschade en aankleding interieur; in 1965 aan advocaat Puissant, die 
2 CONC het huis ten slotte verkocht aan de stad Oudenaarde in 1976, die er onder andere een (restauratie)atelier voor wandtapijten inrichtte.
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id=6000000189420103825&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT English translation by DeepL:History:  Former mansion built on the site of the old pawn shop or Lombard house of Pamele (until the end of the 15th century?). Mentioned in 1519 as "Hof van Schorisse", home of city governor Charles 
2 CONC I de Lalaing, lord of Schorisse, and his wife Jacqueline van Gavere, widow of Jan van Luxemburg. Later called the "House of Luxembourg", inhabited by the son of the above, Philippe de Lalaing. Also called "Hotel van Egmont", the c
2 CONC onference deed of 1714, after the inhabitants in the fourth quarter of the 16th century, Guillaume de Lalaing and his wife Marie Christine van Egmont, daughter of Count Lamoraal van Egmont. Finally also called "Hotel de Berlaimont
2 CONC ", after the daughter of Philippe de Lalaing, Marguerite, who married Florent, Count of Berlaimont. At the end of the 17th century, the estate became the property of the de Kerckhove family, lords of Etikhove and Ladeuze. Successi
2 CONC ve sales; in 1868, among others, to the Boulez family, who sold the Oudenaarde tapestry with which the salons were covered to the English royal house; in 1919 to surgeon De Meulemeester, restoration of war damage and decoration of
2 CONC  the interior; in 1965 to lawyer Puissant, who finally sold the house to the city of Oudenaarde in 1976, which set up a (restoration) studio for tapestries there, among other things.
2 CONT __________
2 CONT As for the mother of '''Philippe DE LALAING''', the family and the writers are not in agreement in this respect; some indicate Marie of Austria, queen of Hungary, without worrying in the least about chronology (1); others prefer M
2 CONC arguerite of Austria, without being able to invoke in their favor anything, absolutely nothing, not even the probability (2).
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) Margarita of Austria, Queen of Hungary, sister of Charles V, was born in Brussels in 1503.
2 CONT (2) Margaret of Austria, daughter of Emperor Maximilian and Mary of Burgundy, was born in 1480. At the time of the birth of Philippe, bastard of LALAING, she was married to Philippe le-Beau, Duke of Savoy.
2 CONT
2 CONT It results from the quarters which were formerly on an old funeral table of Antoinette DE LALAING, wife of Gerard de Seclyn, and daughter of Philippe the bastard, that the mother of this one was of the house of Luxembourg; and old
2 CONC  manuscripts of genealogies indicate Isabeau de Luxembourg, bastard daughter of Ferdinand de Luxembourg, lord of Hautbourdin, and of Jacqueline de Bruaen.
2 CONT
2 CONT ''Dictionnaire généalogique des familles nobles du Royaume de Belgique - F.V.Goethals 1849 TIII''
