2 CONT '''From Gemini AI'''" (''Maybe not the same person?'')
2 CONT De Facuwez" (often seen as de Facuwez) refers to a noble title and family name historically associated with the Duchy of Brabant, a region that today encompasses parts of Belgium and the Netherlands.  
2 CONT Based on historical records, here is the breakdown of who or what it is:
2 CONT '''1. A Marquisate and Lordship'''
2 CONT Historically, Facuwez was a lordship and later a marquisate.
2 CONT • The Marquisate of Facuwez: It had its own coat of arms, which has been documented in genealogical works such as Supplement aux Trophees tant sacres que profanes de Duche de Brabant (1726) by Christophorus Butkens.  
2 CONT • Locations: Records link the name to various locations in Belgium, including Obaix, Ittre, and Rèves.  
2 CONT '''2. Notable Individuals'''
2 CONT Several historical figures bore the name as part of their titles or surnames:
2 CONT • **Guillaume-Philippe, Marquess of Herzelles (1642–1698): He was a magistrate and statesman in the Habsburg Netherlands and held the title of Lord of Facuwez.  
2 CONT • **Josse de Facuwez (died 1569): A historical figure associated with legal or administrative documents in Leuven and Antwerp.  
2 CONT • **Gertrude de Facuwez de Becquevord (1329–1378): A noblewoman from the 14th century buried in Rèves, Belgium.  
2 CONT • Thierry d'Ittre de Facuwez: A member of the nobility from the same era, indicating the family's long-standing presence in the Brabant region.  
2 CONT '''3. Surname Context'''
2 CONT The name often appears in compound forms, such as d'Ittre de Facuwez or de Facuwez de Becquevord, signifying the union of different estates or noble houses through marriage and inheritance.  
2 CONT Would you like more information on the specific genealogy of this family or the history of the Duchy of Brabant?
2 CONT
2 CONT Marguerite de la Viesville (often referred to as Marguerite de la Viesville de Facuwez) was a 17th-century noblewoman from the de la Viesville family, a lineage belonging to the nobility of Artois and the Spanish Netherlands.
2 CONT Here are the key details regarding her life and genealogical significance:
2 CONT '''1. Alliance with the de Facuwez Family'''
2 CONT She is primarily known for her marriage to Jean-Baptiste de Facuwez, who held the titles of Lord of Facuwez, Ittre, and Haut-Ittre. Through this marriage, she became the Lady of Facuwez.
2 CONT '''2. Descent and the Herzelles Family'''
2 CONT She was the mother of a very important historical figure: Guillaume-Philippe, Marquess of Herzelles (1642–1698).
2 CONT • Guillaume-Philippe was an influential statesman and magistrate, holding high-ranking positions in the administration of the Spanish Netherlands (notably as Chancellor of Brabant).
2 CONT • It was through him that the lordship of Facuwez remained central to the titles of the Herzelles family for several generations.
2 CONT '''3. Context of the de la Viesville Family'''
2 CONT The de la Viesville family to which she belonged was an ancient house whose members often held military positions or served as bailiffs in the Artois region. Their coat of arms is generally described as: argent, a fess gules, char
2 CONC ged with three annulets or (a silver shield with a red horizontal band containing three gold rings).
2 CONT '''4. Social Role'''
2 CONT As a noblewoman of her time, Marguerite de la Viesville played a role in managing the seignurial estates of Facuwez and Ittre, particularly in supporting local religious institutions. She is mentioned in various genealogical archi
2 CONC ves of Brabant as a central figure linking the de la Viesville, de Facuwez, and de Herzelles families.
