1 NOTE {geni:about_me} * See [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jean_de_Ligne,_Duke_of_Aremberg '''Wikipedia...''']
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean de Ligne, Duke of Arenberg (ca. 1525 – 1568) was Baron of Barbançon, founder of the House of Arenberg and stadtholder of the Dutch provinces of Friesland, Groningen, Drenthe and Overijssel from 1549 until his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was the son of Louis de Ligne, Baron of Barbançon from the House of Ligne and Maria of Bergen, Lady of Zevenbergen (1503–1566).
2 CONT
2 CONT Jean de Ligne belonged to the closest circles around Charles V and was made a Knight in the Order of the Golden Fleece in 1546.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1549 he became stadtholder of the Northern provinces of Friesland, Groningen, Drenthe and Overijssel.
2 CONT
2 CONT By his marriage to Marguerite de la Marck-Arenberg, sister of Robert Fleuranges III de la Marck who died without children, he became the founder of the third House of Arenberg.
2 CONT
2 CONT He participated in the campaign in France and distinguished himself in the Battle of St. Quentin (1557) where he, together with Henry V, Duke of Brunswick-Lüneburg, led the left wing of the infantry in the final attack against the
2 CONC  French.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the start of the rebellion he distanced himself of his good friend William the Silent, Lamoral, Count of Egmont and Philip de Montmorency, Count of Hoorn, and remained loyal to the King Philip II of Spain.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was unable to stop the spread of Protestantism in his Northern provinces, but succeeded in 1567 to keep them loyal to the Crown without bloodshed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Back south, he joined the army under the Duke of Alva, but objected against the arrests of Egmont and Hoorn.
2 CONT
2 CONT When Louis and Adolf of Nassau (brothers of William I of Orange) invaded Groningen, he was sent back by Alva to repulse this army.
2 CONT
2 CONT There he was killed in the Battle of Heiligerlee on May 23, 1568. Cardinal Granvelle described his death as a great loss for the Catholic faith and the King.
2 CONT
2 CONT Arenberg was buried in the Saint Catherine Church in Zevenbergen, and his remains were moved in 1614 to the family vault in Enghien.
2 CONT
2 CONT He had seven children and was succeeded by his eldest son Charles de Ligne, 2nd Prince of Arenberg.
