1 NAME Nicolaas (Colard) /van Halewijn/ Heer van Boesinghe, Seigneur de Bouzinghen, Lord of the Zwevegem
2 GIVN Nicolaas (Colard)
2 SURN van Halewijn
2 NSFX Heer van Boesinghe, Seigneur de Bouzinghen, Lord of the Zwevegem
2 _MARNM van Halewijn
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 1450
1 DEAT
2 DATE 23 DEC 1528
1 OCCU Heer van Boesinghe (Seigneur de Bouzinghen); Lord of the Zwevegem Cadet Patrimony
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} ''Across the bilingual marches of the Burgundian state, a single lord could bear a solemn baptismal name in a parish register, a courtly diminutive in a ducal cartulary, and a sharp descriptive by-name in a Flemish
2 CONC  landbook.''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Nicolaas "Colard" van Halewijn, Heer van Boesinghe (Seigneur de Bouzinghen); Lord of the Zwevegem'''
2 CONT
2 CONT (Verified Generation 19)
2 CONT '''I. VITAL SUMMARY'''
2 CONT Birth Name: Nicolaas (Nicolas) van Halewijn / de Halluin
2 CONT Known Aliases / Epithets: Colard (Colart) / Callewaert ("De Kale" / The Bald)
2 CONT Titles: Heer van Boesinghe (Seigneur de Bouzinghen); Lord of the Zwevegem Cadet Patrimony
2 CONT True Generation Count: Generation 19 (anchored from Wauthier III)
2 CONT Life Dates: c. 1450 – † 23 December 1528
2 CONT Active Career (Floruit): fl. c. 1475 – 1528 (spanning his adulthood through his public service under the Habsburgs until his death)
2 CONT Status: High Noble Patriarch; Lord of Boesinghe
2 CONT Strategic Role: Northern Territorial Anchor (Establishing the senior cadet lordship of Boesinghe in West Flanders)
2 CONT Parents: Jan IV van Halewijn, Lord of Zwevegem & Jacqueline de Ghistelles (Jacoba van Gistel)
2 CONT Primary Seat: Kasteel van Boesinghe (Boezinge, West Flanders)
2 CONT
2 CONT '''II. MARRIAGE AND ALIGNMENT'''
2 CONT Nicolaas van Halewijn married Johanna (Jeanne) van Stavele, Dame de Boesinghe, daughter of the ancient West Flemish house of Stavele.
2 CONT Through this strategic union, Nicolaas brought the important lordship and domain of Boesinghe (Boezinghe) directly into the Halewijn family network. The marriage secured a key territorial anchor north of Ypres, linking the high co
2 CONC urt influence of his brothers with the wealthy agricultural fiefs of the Yser valley.
2 CONT Children's Bloc (Issue)
2 CONT Jan VI van Halewijn, Heer van Boesinghe: Primary son and heir; succeeded to the lordship of Boesinghe and continued the senior cadet line.
2 CONT Marguerite van Halewijn: Dowered into the regional Flemish nobility; married into the House of van Vaernewyck.
2 CONT [Additional Issue]: Secondary cartularies note cadet issue holding appurtenant lease rights across the Boesinghe and Zwevegem dependency rolls.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''III. NARRATIVE BIOGRAPHY'''
2 CONT Nicolaas van Halewijn was born into the heart of the Burgundian Netherlands at Kasteel van Zwevegem, a senior cadet son of Jan IV van Halewijn and Jacqueline de Ghistelles. Raised during an era of unprecedented territorial consoli
2 CONC dation under Duke Philip the Good, Nicolaas came of age surrounded by the legal, military, and diplomatic duties that defined his family's high standing in Flanders.
2 CONT
2 CONT His marriage to Johanna van Stavele marked a pivotal expansion for the Halewijn dynasty. By acquiring the lordship of Boesinghe through his wife's inheritance, Nicolaas established himself as a major territorial lord in his own ri
2 CONC ght. As Lord of Boesinghe, he managed the administrative, judicial, and agricultural rights of the estate, serving as a vital bridge between the urban authorities of Ypres and the ducal court administration in Lille.
2 CONT
2 CONT Throughout his mature years, Nicolaas maintained close ties with his prolific siblings, coordinating family interests alongside his brothers Joost II (Lord of Zwevegem), Roger (Lord of Quenenghem), and Thierry (Grand Bailiff of Fl
2 CONC anders). He navigated the volatile transition following the fall of the Valois-Burgundy dukes, preserving the integrity of the Boesinghe patrimony and passing a secure, prestigious estate down to his primary heir, Jan VI.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''IV. THE REST OF THE STORY: THE GHOST BROTHERS OF BOESINGHE'''
2 CONT In the dusty pages of 18th-century heraldic surveys, one often finds three distinct brothers listed for this generation: Nicolaas, Colard, and Callewaert van Halewijn—all conveniently recorded as marrying the exact same woman, Joh
2 CONC anna van Stavele!
2 CONT
2 CONT This bizarre "triple marriage" was born not of medieval scandal, but of a complete linguistic misunderstanding across the Franco-Flemish border.
2 CONT
2 CONT During the 15th century, the Burgundian administration operated across two languages: Middle Dutch in local Flemish landbooks, and Picard French at court. In French cartularies, Nicolas was routinely written in its natural courtly
2 CONC  short-form, Colard or Colart. Meanwhile, in local West Flemish municipal registers, neighbors knew him by a vivid personal physical epithet: Callewaert—derived from the Middle Dutch cale ("bald") plus the descriptive suffix -aert
2 CONC  (literally "The Bald One", equivalent to modern Dutch De Kale or French Le Chauve).
2 CONT
2 CONT When later genealogists compiled printed indexes, they misread these entries as three separate individuals. 
2 CONT
2 CONT They took the baptismal name (Nicolaas), the French court diminutive (Colard), and the Flemish physical nickname (Callewaert), split them into three "brothers," and duplicated Johanna van Stavele’s profile across all three. In rea
2 CONC lity, there was only ever one Lord of Boesinghe—a single historical figure whose full administrative footprint spanned both sides of the linguistic divide.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''V. HERALDRY & ALLIANCE SHIELD'''
2 CONT * House of Halewijn (Paternal): Argent, three lions rampant sable, armed, langued, and crowned or.
2 CONT * House of Stavele (Marital): Ermine, a fess gules (or regional variant quarterings of Stavele-Boesinghe).
2 CONT * Alliance Shield: Impaled arms displaying Halewijn (three crowned black lions on silver) on the dexter alongside Stavele (Ermine, a fess gules) on the sinister.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VI. DOCUMENTS AND EVIDENCE'''
2 CONT * P. Anselme de Sainte-Marie: Histoire Généalogique et Chronologique de la Maison Royale de France, Tome III (1733), pp. 904–920 (Seigneurs d'Hallwin et du Gavre - Branche des Seigneurs de Bouzinghen).
2 CONT * Bibliothèque Nationale de France (BnF): Gallica Digital Library, Fol-Lm3-398 A (1), entries for the Lordship of Boesinghe (Bouzinghen).
2 CONT * Flemish Leenregisters & Cartularies of Boesinghe: Municipal landbooks and feudal relief records detailing the succession of Nicolaas van Halewijn and Johanna van Stavele.
2 CONT * Ghistelles & Stavele Dynastic Marriage Settlements: Notarial records detailing the transfer of dower rights for the estate of Boesinghe.
2 CONT * De Vegiano: Nobiliaire des Pays-Bas et du Comté de Bourgogne, compilations recording the lineage of the House of Halewijn-Boesinghe.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VII. CURATOR DISCLAIMER'''
2 CONT <span style="color:red; font-weight:bold;">DISCLAIMER: This profile represents True Generation 19 (son of Jan IV van Halewijn and Jacqueline de Ghistelles). Do not renumber this profile based on secondary French calendar indexes. 
2 CONC Critical Curation & Merge Note: Nicolaas van Halewijn, "Colard" van Halewijn, and "Callewaert" van Halewijn represent a single historical personage. "Colard" is the Picard/French diminutive of Nicolaas, while "Callewaert" is a Mid
2 CONC dle Dutch physical epithet (the bald one / De Kale). Secondary profiles created under these aliases wearing the marriage to Johanna van Stavele should be merged directly into this profile. Verify all primary landbook entries again
2 CONC st Flemish cartularies. Notice: AI-assisted biographical summary. Generative AI models can make mistakes, hallucinate details, or conflate generational data; readers are advised to check and verify against primary archival sources
2 CONC .</span>
