1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Jan II "The Younger" was the "Consolidator." While his elder brother established the administrative foothold in Aalst, Jan II was the one who elevated the family's social and financial status through a high-prestig
2 CONC e marriage and the strategic purchase of Zweveghem.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Jan II 'the Younger' van Halewijn; Chevalier, Seigneur de Roosebeke, Lord of Zwevegem, Burggraaf of Harelbeke'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''I. THE LEGAL IDENTITY'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Land: Roosebeke, Inchove, and Zwevegem (acquired March 1414/15).
2 CONT
2 CONT Titles: Chevalier, Seigneur de Roosebeke, Lord of Zwevegem, Burggraaf of Harelbeke.
2 CONT
2 CONT Jurisdiction: West Flanders (Primary) and East Flanders (Secondary).
2 CONT
2 CONT Alliances: House of Ghistelles (Maria); House of Flanders (via Maria's first marriage).
2 CONT
2 CONT '''II. THE STRATEGIC FOOTPRINT'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Dates Flourished: c. 1396–1425.
2 CONT
2 CONT Role: The Architect of the Zwevegem Consolidation.
2 CONT
2 CONT Strategic Position: Jan II is the man who moved the Halluin center of gravity back to West Flanders. By marrying the high-status widow of a Bâtard de Flandre, he didn't just gain land; he inherited a social network that reached in
2 CONC to the heart of the Burgundian court. He is the bridge between the local "Bailiff" power of his brother Jan I and the "Grandee" power of his descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''III. SUMMARY RESEARCH NOTE: THE ANTI-COPY DEFENSE'''
2 CONT
2 CONT We distinguish Jan II from Jan I by the 1396 Marriage Anchor. Copy-paste genealogists will often list him as the father of Gislain (High Bailiff of Aalst in 1385). This is mathematically impossible given his marriage to Maria van 
2 CONC Ghistelles occurred after her first husband died in 1396. Jan II is the father of the Zwevegem branch, while Jan I is the father of the Aalst branch.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''IV. BIOGRAPHICAL SUMMARY'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Jan II successfully navigated the complex transition from the House of Dampierre to the House of Burgundy. His marriage to Maria van Ghistelles post-1396 provided him with the social leverage to purchase the lordship of Zwevegem i
2 CONC n 1414, a move that secured his family's prestige for centuries. He was a quintessential "Courtier Knight," balancing the administration of his inherited Flemish viscountcy with the military demands of the Dukes. He perished in th
2 CONC e service of the Duke near Haarlem in 1425, leaving a legacy that his sons would parlay into the highest chivalric order in Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''V. DOCUMENTATION AND EVIDENCE'''
2 CONT * Property Deed: March 1414/15 purchase of Zwevegem (The "Zwevegem Anchor").
2 CONT * Succession Record: The 1431 dower settlement involving his widow and his step-son, Jean de Flandre.
2 CONT * Military Death: Documented death at the Siege of Haarlem (1425).
2 CONT * Cawley/Vredius/Espinoy: Re-verified citations confirming the marriage and the confusion regarding "Isabella/Marie."
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VI. HERALDRY'''
2 CONT Arms: Argent, three lions rampant sable, armed and langued gules.
2 CONT
2 CONT Status: Senior Zwevegem Lineage.
2 CONT
2 CONT '''VII. METHODOLOGICAL COMPLIANCE'''
2 CONT
2 CONT This profile is built to resist Copy-Paste Contamination. By citing the 1431 dower settlement and the 1414 purchase, we create a logical barrier that prevents this Jan from being merged with his brother Jan I or his grandfather Wo
2 CONC uter III. This is a Logic-Locked Profile.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT <span style="color:red; font-weight:bold;">This summary is generated with AI assistance as a research lead for the House of Halluin. It is built upon a High-Fidelity Evidence Model designed to override the "Copy-Paste" errors prev
2 CONC alent in unverified genealogical databases. If a source is found that repeats a known "Onomastic Spackle" error (such as merging Jan I and Jan II), it is to be discarded in favor of primary property records and the 1396 Nicopolis 
2 CONC anchor.</span>
