2 CONT * https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/109916600/joan-douglas shows 22 June 1498
2 CONT * There is a cast aluminium plate located adjacent to their effigies: THE MORTON MONUMENT … The Earl died in c1493 and the Countess in c1498.
2 CONT * https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joan_Stewart%2C_Countess_of_Morton: “The Countess Joanna died in 1493, predeceasing her husband, James, by four months.” [2]
2 CONT **2. The Scots Peerage, Founded on Wood's Edition of Sir Robert Douglas's Peerage of Scotland, Vol. VI, ed. James Balfour Paul (Edinburgh: David Douglas, 1909), p. 356
2 CONT * https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/Stewart-1199 “ Joan died on 22 June 1493, just four months before her husband.[5] 
2 CONT ** 5. Cokayne, George Edward. The Complete Peerage; or, A history of the House of lords and all its members from the earliest times. London: The St. Catherine Press, Ltd. (1936), vol. 9, p. 287.
2 CONT ** Douglas Richardson states that Joan was living 16 Oct 1486 but died before 1490.[1] Alison Weir concurs that Joan died sometime after 16 October 1486 but notes only that she was buried in Dalkeith Church and does not hazard a g
2 CONC uess as to the date of her death.[3] It is not certain what source Cokayne's information is based upon.
2 CONT **1.  Douglas Richardson. Royal Ancestry: A Study in Colonial and Medieval Families, 5 vols, ed. Kimball G. Everingham (Salt Lake City: the author, 2013), vol. 1, p. 659 BRUS 12.iii. Joan Stewart.
