1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *'''Maud Greystoke1,2
2 CONT *'''F, #37395, b. circa 1408, d. before 24 August 1418
2 CONT *Father      Sir John Greystoke, 4th Baron Greystoke, Governor of Roxburgh Castle1,2 b. c 1390, d. 8 Aug 1436
2 CONT *Mother     Elizabeth Ferrers1,2 b. c 1393, d. 1434
2 CONT *'''     Maud Greystoke was born circa 1408 at of Yorkshire, England.1 She married Sir John Scrope, 4th Lord Scrope of Masham, Treasurer of England, son of Sir Stephen le Scrope, 2nd Lord Scrope of Masham and Margery Welles, circa
2 CONC  1410; No issue.1,2 Maud Greystoke died before 24 August 1418 at of Masham, Yorkshire, England.1
2 CONT *'''Family      Sir John Scrope, 4th Lord Scrope of Masham, Treasurer of England b. c 1388, d. 15 Nov 1455
2 CONT *Citations
2 CONT * 1.[S5] Douglas Richardson, Plantagenet Ancestry, p. 254.
2 CONT * 2.[S5] Douglas Richardson, Plantagenet Ancestry, p. 363.
2 CONT *From: http://our-royal-titled-noble-and-commoner-ancestors.com/p1245.htm#i37395
2 CONT *____________________
2 CONT *'''John Scrope, 4th Baron Scrope of Masham (c.1388 – 15 November 1455) was an English peer, Privy Councillor and Treasurer of England.
2 CONT *He was the fourth son of Stephen le Scrope, 2nd Baron Scrope of Masham and Margery, daughter of John Welles, 4th Lord Welles. He inherited his title in 1415 when his elder brother Henry Scrope, 3rd Baron Scrope of Masham was exec
2 CONC uted for his part in the Southampton Plot.[1]
2 CONT *In 1424 he was knighted, made a Privy Councillor and appointed to Commissions of the Peace of Essex, Lincolnshire, Nottinghamshire and Yorkshire. He was on the Council of Regency for the young Henry VI. In 1426 he had the attaind
2 CONC er on his title reversed, bought back the Scrope lands confiscated (and granted to other knights in the meantime) following his brother's execution, and was summoned (restored to the Barony) to the House of Lords.[1]
2 CONT *In 1428 he acted as an Ambassador to the Pope, the King of Spain and the King of The Romans, and then to Scotland in 1429. In 1432 he was appointed Lord High Treasurer of England (until 1433). He again acted as ambassador, to the
2 CONC  Grand Master of the Order of St John of Jerusalem in Rhodes in 1435 and the Archbishop of Cologne in 1439.
2 CONT *He died 15 November 1455 and was buried in the Scrope Chapel in York Minster.''' He had married twice; firstly Maud Greystoke''', daughter of Sir John Greystoke and secondly Elizabeth Chaworth, the daughter of Sir Thomas Chaworth
2 CONC , of Wiverton, Nottinghamshire, with whom he had three sons and five daughters. he was succeeded by his third son Thomas, later 5th Baron Scrope.[1]
2 CONT *From: http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Scrope,_4th_Baron_Scrope_of_Masham
2 CONT *________________________
2 CONT *'''Matilda GREYSTOKE
2 CONT *'''Born: 1426
2 CONT *Father: John GREYSTOKE (6º B. Greystoke of Greystoke)
2 CONT *Mother: Elizabeth FERRERS
2 CONT *'''Married: Son SCROPE
2 CONT *From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/GREYSTOKE.htm#Matilda GREYSTOKE1
2 CONT *_____________________
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sir John Scrope, 4th Lord Scrope of Masham, Treasurer of England Ambassador to Spain and Scotland. He was born circa 1388 at of Upsal, Yorkshire, England; Age 30 in 1418.22,5,11,17 He married Maud Greystoke, daughter of Sir John G
2 CONC reystoke, 4th Baron Greystoke, Governor of Roxburgh Castle and Elizabeth Ferrers, circa 1410; No issue.
