2 NOTE Thirty-One Generations "A Thousand Years of Percies and Pierces from 972 to 1948, (p. 18)
3 CONT
3 CONT A son of the earl, SIR RALPH PERCY (17) (1428-1464) was seneschal of his father's court at Alnwick. He died on the battlefield of Hedgeley Moor in April 1464. Percy's Cross was erected there to honor his gallantry when he refused 
3 CONC to save his skin by fleeing, at the expense of "the bird in his bosom".
3 CONT
3 CONT bird-in-the-bosom 
3 CONT Noun 
3 CONT (idiomatic, UK, dated) A secret pledge that one makes for another. https://www.yourdictionary.com/bird-in-the-bosom
3 CONT
3 CONT bird in (one's) bosom 
3 CONT obsolete An oath of faith, servitude, piety, or conscience that is kept guarded in one's heart, mind, and spirit. Truly, my lord, I am at your service and loyal to your name, and I shall guard the bird in my bosom till my last bre
3 CONC ath. <a href="https://idioms.thefreedictionary.com/bird+in+bosom&quot;&gt;bird in (one&#39;s) bosom</a>
3 CONT
3 CONT Ralph's son Peter (18), who was born in 1447 was standard bearer for Richard III at the Battle of Bosworth Field in 1485.
3 CONT
3 CONT Peters son, RICHARD, (19) founded Pearce Hall in York.
3 CONT
3 CONT His son Richard, Jr. (20) changed the family name from Percy to Pierce (also Peirce and Pearce).
