1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Lady Ann Percy''' (d. 5 Jul 1522) was the daughter of Sir Henry Percy, 2nd Earl of Northumberland and Eleanor Neville.  She was buried on  9 July 1522 at St. Michael's, Westminster Abbey, Westminster, Middlesex,
2 CONC  England beside her 3rd husband, Hugh Vaughan, “at a great age.”
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Family 
2 CONT Anne Percy Married 
2 CONT
2 CONT * (1) bef. 14 Oct 1460 Sir Thomas Hungerford, (d 1469, be headed as a traitor), de jure 4th Baron Hungerford.
2 CONT * (2) before 24 June 1470 to Sir Laurence Raynsford (d. 1490) 
2 CONT * (3) between 18 September 1490 and December 1493 to Sir Hugh Vaughan (d. 1536).
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Children
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Anne Percy and Thomas Hungerford
2 CONT * Elizabeth Hungerford 
2 CONT * Mary Hungerford, b c 1468,  married 1) Edward Hastings 2) Richard Sachaverell
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Anne Percy and Laurence Raynsford
2 CONT
2 CONT * said to have had issue 
2 CONT
2 CONT Children of Anne Percy and Hugh Vaughan 
2 CONT
2 CONT * are reported, but seems an error, given her age at their marriage 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT === Disputed origins 
2 CONT Medlands places this Anne Percy as the daughter of Henry Percy and Eleanor Neville, so, the sister of the Henry Percy who married Eleanor Poynings, rather than his daughter.
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________________
2 CONT '''There is a issue with the parentage of Anne Percy, wife of Thomas Hungerford, some sources have her parents as Eleanor Neville & Henry Percy, other sources have Eleanor Poynings & Henry Percy (son of Eleanor Neville & Henry Per
2 CONC cy). Sources have Anne Percy wife of Thomas Hungerford also married Lawrence Raynsford & Sir Hugh Vaughan.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT ________________________
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Source For Birth Place Change:'''
2 CONT
2 CONT http://archiver.rootsweb.ancestry.com/th/read/GEN-MEDIEVAL/2002-03/1016370839
2 CONT
2 CONT The remaining question is where Anne was born. Collins's Peerage (vol. 2, p.291, 1812 edn) says that she "was born at Dugnanis [note: so it seems written in Cavell's Roll, but is thought to mean Dunganess in Scotland]."
2 CONT
2 CONT SEE - http://www.medievalgenealogy.org.uk/cp/hungerford.shtml
2 CONT I can't find anywhere called Dunganess in Scotland. There's a Dungeness in Kent, but it doesn't seem a likely place for the birth of the daughter of an earl of Northumberland.
2 CONT
2 CONT More probably, the place referred to is in fact Dagenham, in Essex, where the 2nd earl held a manor known as "Dagenhams and Cockerels". A Google search shows that this was quite often spelt "Dagnams". So I should think that Collin
2 CONC s's got to "Dugnanis" by misreading the first "a" as a "u", and the "m" as "ni" (in many 15th-century documents "m" and "ni" would be completely interchangeable readings).
2 CONT
2 CONT If that's the correct solution, I'm not sure whether it helps with the chronology. The Complete Peerage says that in 1443 the earl bound himself (briefly) to withdraw no more than 7 miles from London, "save to his manor of Dagenha
2 CONC m, Essex", which perhaps implies that was his usual residence near London.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Genealogies 
2 CONT http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/PERCY.htm#Anne PERCY2
2 CONT
2 CONT * Anne PERCY
2 CONT * Born: 3 Feb 1444, Dunganess
2 CONT * Died: 5 Jul 1522, London, England
2 CONT * Buried: 9 Jul 1522, St. Margaret, Westminster, Middlesex, England 
2 CONT * Father: Henry PERCY (3º E. Northumberland)
2 CONT * Mother: Eleanor POYNINGS (B. Poynings)
2 CONT * Married 1: Thomas HUNGERFORD (Sir) BEF 16 Oct 1460 / 1465, Warkworth, Northumberland, England
2 CONT * Children:
2 CONT * **1. Mary HUNGERFORD (B. Hungerford and Moleyns)
2 CONT * Married: 2: Laurence RAINSFORD (Sir) ABT 1473
2 CONT * Married: 3: Thomas VAUGHN (Sir) ABT 1483
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://thepeerage.com/p14716.htm#i147159
2 CONT
2 CONT Anne Percy was the daughter of Henry Percy, 2nd Earl of Northumberland and Eleanor de Poynings, Baroness de Poynings.1,2 She married Thomas Hungerford, son of Robert Hungerford, 3rd Lord Hungerford and Elizabeth Moleyns, before 16
2 CONC  October 1460.2. From before 16 October 1460, her married name became Hungerford.1
2 CONT
2 CONT Child of Anne Percy and Thomas Hungerford
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.Mary Hungerford, Baroness Botreaux+1 b. c 1468, d. b 10 Jul 1533
2 CONT
2 CONT Citations
2 CONT *1.[S6] G.E. Cokayne; with Vicary Gibbs, H.A. Doubleday, Geoffrey H. White, Duncan Warrand and Lord Howard de Walden, editors, The Complete Peerage of England, Scotland, Ireland, Great Britain and the United Kingdom, Extant, Extin
2 CONC ct or Dormant, new ed., 13 volumes in 14 (1910-1959; reprint in 6 volumes, Gloucester, U.K.: Alan Sutton Publishing, 2000), volume II, page 243. Hereinafter cited as The Complete Peerage.
2 CONT *2.[S6] Cokayne, and others, The Complete Peerage, volume VI, page 621.
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Hungerford_of_Rowden
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Thomas Hungerford of Rowden (died 1469), the eldest son of Robert Hungerford, 3rd Baron Hungerford, lived chiefly at Rowden, near Chippenham. After giving some support to Edward IV and the Yorkists he joined in Warwick's consp
2 CONC iracy to restore Henry VI in 1469, was attainted, and was executed at Salisbury. He was buried in the chapel of Farleigh Castle.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Thomas married Anne, daughter of Henry Percy, 3rd Earl of Northumberland, who married two husbands after his death—Sir Lawrence Raynesford and Sir Hugh Vaughan—and, dying on 5 July 1522, was buried in St. Margaret's Church, We
2 CONC stminster.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Thomas left by Anne an only child, Mary, 4th Baroness Hungerford, who became the ward of William, Lord Hastings, and in 1480 married Sir Edward (afterwards Lord) Hastings, her guardian's son. The attainders on her father and g
2 CONC randfather were reversed in her favour in 1485, and her husband was summoned to Parliament as Lord Hungerford. George Hastings, 1st Earl of Huntingdon, was her son.
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT *Lee, Sidney Dictionary of National Biography (1885–1900) Volume 28, p. 257 
2 CONT *Dugdale's Baronage ;
2 CONT *Hoare's Hungerfordiana ;
2 CONT *Letters, &c., of Henry VIII;
2 CONT *Materials for the Reign of Henry VII (Eolls Ser.);
2 CONT *Paston Letters, passim, ed. Gairdner
2 CONT *Hoare's Mod. "Wiltshire, Heytesbury Hundred"
2 CONT *Collinson's Somerset, iii. 355
2 CONT * "Robert Hungerford". Dictionary of National Biography, 1885–1900​. London: Smith, Elder & Co.
2 CONT <br/>
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Sir Hugh Vaughan (? – 28 August 1536)[1] was born in Wales as a commoner to unknown parents, who later became Governor of Jersey (or Captain).
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hugh_Vaughan_(Governor_of_Jersey)_
2 CONT
2 CONT His first, or possibly second, wife was Anne Percy the daughter of Henry Percy, 3rd Earl of Northumberland, and the widow of both Sir Thomas Hungerford of Rowden and Sir Laurence Raynesford.[1] Anne died on 5 July 1522, and someti
2 CONC me after that he married his second, or possibly third, wife who was named Blanche, the daughter of John Castell.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT He had at least nine children. One son named Rowland, and three daughters whose mother was probably Anne,[3] and three sons, and four daughters whose mother was Blanche.
2 CONT
2 CONT Vaughan died on 28 August 1536, and was buried with Anne Percy in the chapel of St Michael in Westminster Abbey.[1] His widow Blanche died on 8 December 1553, and was buried at the church of St Mary Magdalene in Littleton, in Midd
2 CONC lesex (now Surrey).
2 CONT
2 CONT
