2 CONT John Neville, 1st Marquess of Montagu (c. 1431 – 14 April 1471) was a Yorkist leader in the Wars of the Roses, best-known for eliminating Lancastrian resistance in the north of England during the early part of the reign of Edward 
2 CONC IV of England.
2 CONT
2 CONT Montagu was a younger son of Richard Neville, 5th Earl of Salisbury and Alice Montagu, Countess of Salisbury, and a brother of Warwick the Kingmaker.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought at the Battle of Blore Heath and was captured and imprisoned by the Lancastrians. After the Yorkist victory at Northampton he was released, but was captured again at the Second Battle of St Albans.
2 CONT
2 CONT Following his second release from imprisonment, he led the Yorkist forces in the north of England, defeating the Lancastrians at Hedgeley Moor and again at Hexham (both 1464).
2 CONT
2 CONT In reward for driving out the Lancastrians, Montagu was created Earl of Northumberland. This title had long been held by the Percy family, but they were in disgrace. However, when Henry Percy was rehabilitated in 1470, Montagu was
2 CONC  forced to give up the earldom and many important offices in favour of his former foe.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was in compensation created Marquess of Montagu, but without suitable estates to support such a dignity. Now set against Edward IV, he joined his brother Warwick the Kingmaker in the restoration of Henry VI. Montagu returned to
2 CONC  high office in the north, but was killed at the Battle of Barnet.
2 CONT
2 CONT He married Isabel Ingoldsthorpe (1441–1476) and had seven children:
2 CONT
2 CONT     * Anne Neville, married Sir William Stonor-descendant of William de la Pole, 1st Duke of Suffolk
2 CONT     * Isabelle Neville, married Sir William Huddleston, then Sir William Smythe
2 CONT     * Elizabeth Neville, married Sir Henry Wentworth
2 CONT     * John Neville, died sp bef. 1483 (dead at birth)
2 CONT     * Margaret Neville, married Sir John Mortimer, then Charles Brandon, 1st Duke of Suffolk
2 CONT     * George Neville, Duke of Bedford (c. 1457–1483), died sp
2 CONT     * Lucy Neville, married Sir Thomas FitzWilliam of Aldwark, then Sir Anthony Browne.
2 CONT
2 CONT Lucy Neville was the Ancestor of President Franklin Roosevelt and Sir Winston Churchill.
2 CONT
2 CONT Isabel married a year after his death, to Sir William Norreys.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Sir John Neville, 1st and last Marquess of Montagu was invested as a Knight on 25 December 1449 at Greenwich, London, England, by King Henry VI.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought in the Battle of Stamford Bridge in July 1456, fought as a result of the dispute between 1st Lord of Montagu and 1st Lord Egremont.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought in the Battle of Blore Heath on 23 September 1459, where he took part in the Yorkist victory, but was still captured by the Lancastrians.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 20 November 1459 he was attainted.
2 CONT
2 CONT On 10 July 1460 the attainder was reversed.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Chamberlain to King Henry VI.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.) in January 1460/61.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought in the Second Battle of St. Albans on 17 February 1460/61, where he was again captured by the Lancastrians.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was created 1st Lord Montagu [England] on 23 May 1461.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was invested as a Knight, Order of the Garter (K.G.) on 21 March 1461/62.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought in the siege of Bamborough Castle on 24 December 1462, which surrendered to him.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was Chief Steward of the Bishopric of Durham in 1463.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Justice of the Peace (J.P.) for County Durham in 1463.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought in the Battle of Alnwick Castle in January 1462/63. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Warden of the East Marches towards Scotland between 26 May 1463 and July 1470.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was a Commissioner to treat with the Scots for peace on 5 April 1464.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought in the Battle of Hedgeley Moor, Northumberland on 25 April 1464, where he lead the Yorkists to victory, and made possible a 15 year truce with the Scots (preventing the Lancastrians from operating north of the border).
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought in the Battle of Hexham on 25 May 1464, where he finally destroyed the Lancastrians in the North at that stage of the War of the Roses.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was created 1st Earl of Northumberland [England] on 27 May 1464, as a reward for his victories over the Lancastrians.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Guardian of the truce with Scotland on 11 June 1464.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Sheriff of Northumberland on 28 July 1466, for life.
2 CONT
2 CONT He was created 1st Marquess of Montagu [England] on 25 March 1470, in compensation for the loss of the Earldom of Northumberland, although this new title did not come with any estates in support.
2 CONT
2 CONT He resigned as Earl of Northumberland on 25 March 1470 in favour of Henry Percy, whose family customarily held this title.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Warden of the East Marches between 22 October 1470 and 1471.
2 CONT
2 CONT He held the office of Lieutenant in the North on 21 December 1470.
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought in the Battle of Barnet on 14 April 1471.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT House of Neville.
2 CONT
2 CONT Born 1431.
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 19-20
2 CONT
2 CONT In SEP 1459 his father, Richard NEVILLE, Earl of Salisbury, born 1400, was expected to reach Ludlow, leading an armed force from the North to join forces with Richard PLANTAGENET, Duke of York, born 1411, and those soon to come fr
2 CONC om Calais under command of Richard NEVILLE, Earl of Warwick, born 1428. They were ambushed at a place called Blore Heath, to the north of Shrewsbury, by the Queen's [Marguerite d'ANJOU] army. He and his brother Thomas, were taken 
2 CONC captive but his father was able to fight his way free and sent word ahead to warn the Duchess of York, and that he should reach Ludlow by the next night. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: The Sunne in Splendour by Sharon K. PENMAN issued by Macmillan, London.
2 CONT
2 CONT Added by Y. DROST, 25 JUL 2011
