2 CONT '''Margaret NEVILLE (C. Oxford)'''
2 CONT '''Born: ABT 1442, Salisbury, Wiltshire, England'''
2 CONT '''Died: AFT 20 Nov 1506'''
2 CONT Notes: See her Biography.
2 CONT '''Father''': Richard NEVILLE (1° E. Salisbury)
2 CONT '''Mother''': Alice MONTAGUE (C. Salisbury)
2 CONT '''Married: John De VERE (13° E. Oxford) AFT 1459'''
2 CONT '''Children''':
2 CONT 1. George De VERE (d. young)
2 CONT From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/NEVILLE2.htm#Margaret%20NEVILLE%20(C.%20Oxford)
2 CONT '''Margaret NEVILLE'''
2 CONT '''(C. Oxford)'''
2 CONT '''Margaret Neville was the youngest daughter''' of Richard Neville, Earl of Salisbury and his wife Alice Montague. '''She was the sister''' of Richard, "the king maker", first Earl of Warwick; John, first Marquess of Montague; an
2 CONC d George, Archbishop of York. '''Unlike her sisters''', Joan, Countess of Arundel; Cecily Duchess of Warwick and Countess of Worcester; Alice, Baroness Fitzhugh of Ravensworth; Catherine, Baroness Ashby-Zouch, and Eleanor, first w
2 CONC ife of Thomas Stanley, first Earl of Derby; '''would apparently avoid the perilous marriage stakes until she was in her mid 20s. Her husband was loyal Lancastrian John de Vere, 13th Earl of Oxford''', who had already done time in 
2 CONC the tower for his part in a red rose conspiracy. By the summer of 1469 he had been pardoned by Edward IV and upon his release from the tower, took off with his brother in law Warwick to stir up Robin of Redesdale’s northern rebell
2 CONC ion.
2 CONT The ups and downs of the war of the two roses, between the York and the Lancasters, also affected those of the faithful Oxford. With a victorious Edward IV in charge, '''Oxford takes refuge in Scotland from where he writes asking 
2 CONC for the assistance of his wife Margaret: ‘Also ye shall send me in all haste all the ready money ye can make; and as many of my men as can come well horsed, and that they come in divers parcels’.'''
2 CONT '''Presumably Margaret was still living at the family seat of Castle Hedingham in Essex with her only child''' George. '''But by Apr 1472 Margaret was at St Martin’s sanctuary. Her status as the wife of a traitor rendered her vuln
2 CONC erable.'''
2 CONT Meanwhile fearless De Vere continued his fight for the Lancastrian cause. Following his eventual surrender in 1474 he spent the next ten years a prisoner at Hammes Castle. '''For more than fourteen years Margaret lived a life of p
2 CONC enury. It was said she relied upon the charity of others and what ‘she could earn by her needle’. King Edward pardoned her but it was not until 1481 that he granted her £100 a year ‘on account of her poverty’.
2 CONT In 1484 John escaped from his gaolers.''' He quickly joined Henry Tudor and played a significant role in the contender’s victory. '''Margaret and her husband were reinstated at Castle Hedingham but sadly their son had died sometim
2 CONC e during his father’s imprisonment.'''
2 CONT '''Margaret died in 1506. She was well into her 60s, a respectable age for a woman who had experienced the vicissitudes of the long years of war. She was buried before the altar of the Lady Chapel of Colne Priory in Essex where he
2 CONC r husband John joined her seven years later,''' after marriyng a second wife, Elizabeth Scrope. '''Originally alabaster effigies of the couple lie side by said, she with her feet on a winged boar, he with his on a stag. These effi
2 CONC gies were destroyed in the mid 18th century, but a drawing made in 1653 survives, the only known representation of Margaret.'''
2 CONT Sources: Friends of Lydiard Park: https://www.friendsoflydiardpark.org.uk/news/blog-post/margaret-neville-countess-of-oxford/
2 CONT From: http://www.tudorplace.com.ar/Bios/MargaretNeville(COxford).htm
2 CONT _________
2 CONT '''Margaret Neville de Vere'''
2 CONT https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/196980468/margaret-de_vere
2 CONT ______________
2 CONT Richard Neville, 5th Earl of Salisbury KG PC (1400 – 31 December 1460) was an English nobleman and magnate based in northern England who became a key supporter of the House of York during the early years of the Wars of the Roses. 
2 CONC He was the father of Richard Neville, 16th Earl of Warwick, the "Kingmaker".
2 CONT Origins
2 CONT He was born in 1400 at Raby Castle in County Durham, the third son (and tenth child) of Ralph de Neville, 1st Earl of Westmorland, by his second wife, Joan Beaufort, the youngest of the four legitimised children and only daughter 
2 CONC of John of Gaunt, 1st Duke of Lancaster (third surviving son of King Edward III), by his mistress, later wife, Katherine Swynford.
2 CONT etc.
2 CONT Marriage and issue
2 CONT He married Alice Montacute (1407–1462), daughter and heiress of Thomas Montacute, 4th Earl of Salisbury (1388–1428), by whom he had twelve children:
2 CONT Sons
2 CONT *Richard Neville, 16th Earl of Warwick (1428–1471), "The Kingmaker", eldest son and heir, who married Lady Anne Beauchamp and had issue.
2 CONT *Sir Thomas Neville (c. 1429–1460),[6] who was knighted in 1449 and died at the Battle of Wakefield. He was the second husband of Maud Stanhope (30 August 1497), who married firstly Robert Willoughby, 6th Baron Willoughby de Eresb
2 CONC y (died 25 July 1452), and thirdly Sir Gervase Clifton, beheaded 6 May 1471 after the Battle of Tewkesbury.[7]
2 CONT *John Neville, 1st Marquess of Montagu (c. 1431–1471), married Isabel Ingaldesthorpe and had issue.
2 CONT *George Neville (1432–1476), who became Archbishop of York and Chancellor of England.
2 CONT *Ralph Neville (b. 1440 approx.), did not survive infancy
2 CONT *Robert Neville (b. 1446 approx.), did not survive infancy
2 CONT Daughters
2 CONT *Joan Neville (c. 1424–1462), who married William FitzAlan, 16th Earl of Arundel, and had issue.
2 CONT *Cecily Neville (c. 1425–1450), who married Henry Beauchamp, 1st Duke of Warwick (1425–1446), and by him had one daughter, Anne Beauchamp, 15th Countess of Warwick (1444–1449), on whose death her title passed to her paternal aunt,
2 CONC  who in turn had married her maternal uncle Richard Neville (below named).[8]
2 CONT *Alice Neville (c. 1430–1503), who married Henry FitzHugh, 5th Baron FitzHugh. Their daughter, Elizabeth, married William Parr, thus making them great-grandparents of Catherine Parr, sixth wife of King Henry VIII.
2 CONT *Eleanor Neville (c. 1438–before 1472[9]), who married Thomas Stanley, 1st Earl of Derby, and had issue.
2 CONT *Katherine Neville (1442–1504), who married first William Bonville, 6th Baron Harington, and second William Hastings, 1st Baron Hastings, had issue.
2 CONT *'''Margaret Neville (c. 1444–1506), who married John de Vere, 13th Earl of Oxford.'''
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Neville,_5th_Earl_of_Salisbury
2 CONT _____
2 CONT Link
2 CONT * https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/ENGLISH%20NOBILITY%20MEDIEVAL1.htm#RichardVereOxforddied1417B
2 CONT _____________
