1 CONT The Nevilles were the most powerful family in England in the 1460's and Richard Neville's son, another Richard Neville (the "King-Maker") was their greatest member.
1 CONT On 24 August 1453 a quarrel between the Nevilles and thePercies developed into open warfare when a 700-strong force of men-at-arms in the Percy livery fell upon a party of Nevilles, including the Earl of Salisbury, his wife and so
1 CONC ns, returning from a wedding between Thomas Neville, Salisbury's second son, and Matilda, niece and heiress of Ralph, Lord Cromwell. The wedding party was armed (another indication of the state of affairs) and a battle raged acros
1 CONC s the Yorkshire Moors for several hours, with a number of people being killed and many wounded, while the Neville ladies rode for shelter at the manor of Sheriff Hutton. (Source: "The Wars of the Roses", by Robin Neillands)
1 CONT In December 1460 Salisbury was captured in the field at the battle of Wakefield and taken to Pontefract Castle, where he was beheaded the next day, his head mounted over the Micklebar gate of the city of York.
