0 @N6000000000146853163@ NOTE Alice Neville; born circa 1430 of Salisbury, Wiltshire, England,[N]; married Henry FitzHugh III Lord FitzHugh, son of William FitzHugh Lord FitzHugh and Margery de Willoughby; died after 22 Nov 1503[N].
0 @N6000000001783494986@ NOTE Basic Life Information
1 CONT Sir John Neville, 1st and last Marquess of Montagu was born circa 1431. He was the son of Sir Richard de Neville, 5th Earl of Salisbury and Alice Montagu, Countess of Salisbury.
1 CONT
1 CONT Sir John Neville, 1st and last Marquess of Montagu was invested as a Knight on 25 December 1449 at Greenwich, London, England, by King Henry VI. He fought in the Battle of Stamford Bridge in July 1456, fought as a result of the di
1 CONC spute between 1st Lord of Montagu and 1st Lord Egremont. He fought in the Battle of Blore Heath on 23 September 1459, where he took part in the Yorkist victory, but was still captured by the Lancastrians. On 20 November 1459 he wa
1 CONC s attainted.1 On 10 July 1460 the attainder was reversed. He held the office of Chamberlain to King Henry VI.1 He was invested as a Privy Counsellor (P.C.) in January 1460/61. He fought in the Second Battle of St. Albans on 17 Feb
1 CONC ruary 1460/61, where he was again captured by the Lancastrians. He was created 1st Lord Montagu [England] on 23 May 1461. He was invested as a Knight, Order of the Garter (K.G.) on 21 March 1461/62. He fought in the siege of Bambo
1 CONC rough Castle on 24 December 1462, which surrendered to him. He was Chief Steward of the Bishopric of Durham in 1463. He held the office of Justice of the Peace (J.P.) for County Durham in 1463. He fought in the Battle of Alnwick C
1 CONC astle in January 1462/63. He held the office of Warden of the East Marches towards Scotland between 26 May 1463 and July 1470. He was a Commissioner to treat with the Scots for peace on 5 April 1464. He fought in the Battle of Hed
1 CONC geley Moor, Northumberland on 25 April 1464, where he lead the Yorkists to victory, and made possible a 15 year truce with the Scots (preventing the Lancastrians from operating north of the border). He fought in the Battle of Hexh
1 CONC am on 25 May 1464, where he finally destroyed the Lancastrians in the North at that stage of the War of the Roses. He was created 1st Earl of Northumberland [England] on 27 May 1464, as a reward for his victories over the Lancastr
1 CONC ians. He held the office of Guardian of the truce with Scotland on 11 June 1464.5 He held the office of Sheriff of Northumberland on 28 July 1466, for life. He was created 1st Marquess of Montagu [England] on 25 March 1470, in com
1 CONC pensation for the loss of the Earldom of Northumberland, although this new title did not come with any estates in support. He resigned as Earl of Northumberland on 25 March 1470 in favour of Henry Percy, whose family customarily h
1 CONC eld this title. He held the office of Warden of the East Marches between 22 October 1470 and 1471. He held the office of Lieutenant in the North on 21 December 1470. He fought in the Battle of Barnet on 14 April 1471.
1 CONT
1 CONT Marriage and Children
1 CONT He married Isabel Ingaldesthorpe, daughter of Sir Edmund Ingaldesthorpe and Joan Tiptoft, on 25 April 1457. They had the following children:
1 CONT Lucy Neville+ d. c 1534
1 CONT Isabella Neville
1 CONT George Neville, 1st and last Duke of Bedford b. c 1461, d. 4 May 1483
1 CONT Lady Anne Neville+ b. b 1464, d. b 1486
1 CONT Elizabeth Neville b. c 1464, d. Sep 1517
1 CONT Margaret Neville b. 1466, d. 31 Jan 1527/28
1 CONT
1 CONT Death
1 CONT He fought in the Battle of Barnet on 14 April 1471 and was killed in action on 14 April 1471. He was buried at Bisham Abbey, Berkshire, England.
1 CONT <http://thepeerage.com/p23.htm#i227>
