1 CONT Edward IV Plantagenet, King of England was the son of Richard Plantagenet, 3rd Duke of York and Lady Cecily Neville. He was born on 28 April 1442 at Rouen, Caux, France.1 He was baptised at Rouen Cathedral, Rouen, Caux, France. He
1 CONC  married Elizabeth Wydevill <p10165.htm>, daughter of Sir Richard Wydevill, 1st Earl Rivers <p10292.htm> and Jacquetta de Luxembourg <p10197.htm>, on 1 May 1464 at Grafton Regis, Northamptonshire, England.2 He died on 9 April 1483
1 CONC  at age 40 at Westminster Palace, Westminster, London, England.3 He was buried at St. George's Chapel, Windsor, Berkshire, England.  He succeeded to the title of Earl of March between 21 September 1445 and 22 December 1445.1 On 14
1 CONC 48 his name was legally changed to Edward Plantagenet.1 He fought in the Battle of St. Albans on 22 May 1455.1 He held the office of Constable of Bristol Castle on 14 November 1460.1 He succeeded to the title of 4th Duke of York o
1 CONC n 30 December 1460.1 He succeeded to the title of Earl of Ulster on 30 December 1460.4 He succeeded to the title of Earl of Cambridge on 30 December 1460.4 He fought in the Battle of Mortimer's Cross on 2 February 1460/61 at Wigmo
1 CONC re, Herefordshire, England,, where he utterly defeated Earls of Pembroke and Wiltshire.5 He gained the title of King Edward IV of England on 4 March 1461.6 He was crowned King of England on 28 June 1461 at Westminster Abbey, Westm
1 CONC inster, London, England, and styled 'Rex Angliae et Franciae et Dominus Hiberniae.6' He was godfather for Edward Tiptoft, 2nd Earl of Worcester <p267.htm> at his baptism.7 He was deposed as King of England on 3 October 1470.4 He g
1 CONC ained the title of King Edward IV of England on 11 April 1471.4 His marriage to Elizabeth Wydevill was annulled on 25 June 1483 by an Act of Parliament, known as the 'Titulus Regius', because of King Edward's alleged precontract t
1 CONC o Eleanor Butler. The marriage was ultimately recognised as valid in October 1485 by the first Act of Parliament of King Henry VII.2 He has an extensive biography in the Dictionary of National Biography.8  <e61.htm> <e61.htm>
